{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference","id":1225276,"title":"Steel and Iron: What's the difference","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-06-06T08:00:24-07:00","id":1225276,"title":"Steel and Iron: What's the difference","content":"Okay so this is something that's bugged me in Exalted. In exalted the raksha are weak to weapons made of 'iron' but not weapons made of steel or other alloys. Apparently cast iron or wrought iron qualify as 'iron'<br /> <br /> So first, let's discuss real world definitions. <br /> <br /> pure iron: No alloy material. Not quite as bad as making a sword out of lead, but similar. Pure iron is a terrible material. It's soft, it's weak and it would be about as effective a weapon as a butter knife.<br /> <br /> Wrought iron: Extremely low carbon content by weight ~ 0.08% carbon. Also is alloyed with slag (up to 2% by weight). Slag is a byproduct of the transition from iron ore to iron. Better than pure iron, but only because the slag gives it some kind of structure. The carbon here provides some structure but not a whole lot.<br /> <br /> Steel: ~0.1% carbon by weight - 2.1% carbon by weight - Most people know what steel is. What most people don't realize is that 'steel' encompasses thousands of materials with distinct properties based on the exact carbon content and the heat treatment it undergoes. Some steel is also slightly altered by small amount of other alloying materials. In general, higher carbon = stiffer, harder steel while less carbon generally indicates softer steel but due to heat treatment you can get a some steels that invert this. All historical swords (and most other weapons in the iron age and beyond) that comprise iron of any type live in this range.<br /> <br /> Cast iron: 2.1%-4% carbon content by weight - Once again, multiple different types of cast iron with different properties. Pretty much the defining characteristic is that cast iron is hard and brittle. It does not behave as a ductile material. Once again, this range is terrible for making weapons out of. Here, though it's a matter of your sword shattering the first time you use it. This is the same problem as forging a sword out of steel, quenching it and then forgetting to temper it. <br /> <br /> So this is why I have trouble with the whole Raksha are weak to 'iron' but not 'steel' thing. Steel and cast iron are literally just iron+carbon with heat treatment and wrought iron throws in some extra slag for good measure. The range of steel that's closer to pure iron than wrought iron is huge. The range of steel that is closer to pure iron than cast iron is <i>all of it</i>. <br /> <br /> So reality aside, how does this work in Exalted? Because I'd love an explanation and I know too much about real world steel to just brush it aside (I had an entire engineering class dedicated to <i>just </i>steel and cast iron. I'm not a metallurgist, but I'm not a layperson either).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T08:23:11-07:00","id":1225285,"title":"","content":"I don't think it's at all about the chemical purity of iron. At face value, the simple implication of the distinction made by the Iron bane in Ex3, is that it's defined by the practical and historical differences between the iron and steel industries. Culturally, different developments in the treatment and processing of iron gave rise to forms with different qualities and applications over time, and by common understanding <i>this </i>is the line where the game draws a disparity between Iron and Steel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":188,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","user":"YeOfLittleFaith","date":". 06-06-2018, 11:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T08:37:01-07:00","id":1225291,"title":"","content":"Like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar754_2.gif');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"754\" data-vbnamecard=\"754\">Eldagusto</a> said in the Ask the Devs, it is not chemistry.<br /> <br /> It's about a narrative, a fable of how &quot;iron&quot; is anathema to fae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:18:20-07:00","id":1225311,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TGUEIROS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225291#post1225291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's about a narrative, a fable of how &quot;iron&quot; is anathema to fae.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is, that narrative doesn't actually make sense, either in Exalted <i>or</i> when you consider the real-world mythology it's based on. &quot;Steel&quot; as distinct from &quot;wrought iron&quot; or &quot;cast iron&quot; is simply a linguistic quirk of English. Latin, for example, has no such distinction - &quot;Ferrum&quot; was the standard noun for iron <i>and</i> steel there. It's entirely possible for many or most cultures in Creation to call steel something that translates to &quot;hard iron&quot; or &quot;strong iron&quot; or &quot;sword iron&quot; or something. So it can't be just the name of the stuff that's doing it. It can't be a matter of purity, since, as armyofwhispers already pointed out, steel is <i>less</i> adulterated with stuff than other materials that do count, and, importantly, people in Creation would <i>know</i> that. The process of making cast iron, for example, involves adding substantial carbon in the form of coal, charcoal, or similar substances, and I doubt people in Creation would somehow see that as more &quot;pure&quot; than steel, which involves less adding carbon and more working to remove impurities. And it wouldn't be a matter of strength or stability, because, as also observed, pure iron is pretty garbage in both those respects.<br /> <br /> And the real-world narrative doesn't make sense either. &quot;Cold iron&quot;, as a thing distinct from steel, is not a distinction folklore made at all. It basically only dates to fantasy fiction in the 20th century, and particularly RPGs. RPGs probably did it as a combination of wanting to have faeries have their traditional vulnerability to iron but not have that apply to every sword the heroes pick up, and a misunderstanding of the <a href=\"http://www.kiplingsociety.co.uk/poems_coldiron.htm\" target=\"_blank\">Rudyard Kipling poem</a> of that name. The poem basically uses &quot;cold iron&quot; as a poetic description of <i>all</i> iron, but people have sort of assumed it was special. It's like people read Homer's description of the &quot;wine dark sea&quot; and assumed that in RPGs based on Greek mythology, the Aegean was a special sea made of wine. Anyway, the historical folklore that claims faeries are vulnerable to iron makes no distinction between steel or wrought iron or cast iron or any other kind of iron. Burying a knife under your door was supposed to ward off witches and faeries, for example, and a knife blade would have been made from the best steel the people at the time could make.<br /> <br /> In my campaign, I've just declared that the Fair Folk take aggravated damage from any iron weapon, which certainly includes steel swords. I don't think this makes much difference balance-wise, since a) good steel is rare in Creation anyway, and b) aggravated damage doesn't actually make much difference during a fight - it has no mechanical significance besides being harder to heal. A mortal going up against a cataphract or even a hobgoblin is probably still going to lose, they just might inflict some wounds that the fair folk will have a harder time healing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:27:14-07:00","id":1225316,"title":"","content":"The iron vulnerability stems primarily from &quot;Cold Iron&quot;. This is a long established anti-fae charm in a bunch of cultures.<br /> <br /> Since a good deal of fae stories are stone-age old, some history buffs say iron might have such rep because invaders trampled their stone and wood armaments with metal weapons.<br /> <br /> If so, steel would indeed fit similarly, but the advent of steel over other works of iron came at a much later date, where such tradition was already settled enough on a previously established metallurgy.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:31:21-07:00","id":1225318,"title":"","content":"I personally expect that because of the raksha's nature as creatures of <i>story</i>, whatever effect in Creations history or prehistory that made them vulnerable to iron, turned out to be limited to implements that the inhabitants of Creation <i>called</i> iron and not those implements <i>called</i> steel. And probably the language of relevance for that is the unchanging Old Realm, &quot;[t]he native language of the spirits and those that created them, as well as of the Fair Folk.&quot; Thus, metallurgy is only indirectly relevant; it tells you which alloys are properly called &quot;iron&quot; in Old Realm, and which are called &quot;steel&quot;, and that tells you which will affect the Fair Folk.<br /> <br /> Real-world science tries to be mindful of the distinction between the map and the territory. This distinction can be applied as well to raksha, but the difference is that they are entities of the map, not of the territory. Just as focusing on the map in rather than the territory can lead you into trouble in the sciences, focusing on the territory rather than the map will lead you into trouble when dealing with the Fair Folk.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Note that the Old Realm words might not map perfectly on to the English words. Pretty much the only thing that has to be true to match what we know in canon is that for whatever reason, weapons of &quot;iron&quot; are sufficiently inferior in some way to &quot;steel&quot; that the latter is the preferred material for weapons in modern Creation. And of course, Your Creation May Vary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":572,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","user":"Verzio","date":". 06-06-2018, 12:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:43:40-07:00","id":1225323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225316#post1225316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The iron vulnerability stems primarily from &quot;Cold Iron&quot;. This is a long established anti-fae charm in a bunch of cultures.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? Can you point out evidence of this? Because as far as I'm aware, this is purely a modern interpretation of the tales. See Kelly Pedersen's post above<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225316#post1225316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since a good deal of fae stories are stone-age old, some history buffs say iron might have such rep because invaders trampled their stone and wood armaments with metal weapons.</div> </div> </div> </div>No they are not. Medieval Europe was not the stone age. We have literally no idea what stories they told in the stone age as there was no effective way of passing them down.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Synapse</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225316#post1225316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If so, steel would indeed fit similarly, but the advent of steel over other works of iron came at a much later date, where such tradition was already settled enough on a previously established metallurgy.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, no. Steel in weapons and tools came around at just about the same time as iron in general. Mass production of steel didn't occur until way, way later. This is a common misconception though<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:49:58-07:00","id":1225326,"title":"","content":"I like Kelly's view and change. The idea of the iron weakness always made me feel like it was nature vs. civilization. In Exalted, i would assume this being a Wyld vs. stabilized chunk of creation. But cutting steel out of that application gives iron an odd property on its own.<br /> <br /> Additionally, this all ignores the other materials used for weapons. Copper &amp; bronze give a non applicable option and were used for hundreds of years before iron became worthwhile in history. If most weapons are made of these then the iron weakness makes more sense, while heavy steel assumptions make iron odd on its own.<br /> <br /> If we wanted the idea to get blended into Exalted more, maybe the thing needed in iron to do the job is not carbon for steel but instead white jade dust or something from the Imperial Mountain. If part of the idea of the Fae weakness is that most weapons don't do this damage, then something else should get designed in the system to handle it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:50:09-07:00","id":1225327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225318#post1225318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I personally expect that because of the raksha's nature as creatures of <i>story</i>, whatever effect in Creations history or prehistory that made them vulnerable to iron, turned out to be limited to implements that the inhabitants of Creation <i>called</i> iron and not those implements <i>called</i> steel. And probably the language of relevance for that is the unchanging Old Realm, &quot;[t]he native language of the spirits and those that created them, as well as of the Fair Folk.&quot; Thus, metallurgy is only indirectly relevant; it tells you which alloys are properly called &quot;iron&quot; in Old Realm, and which are called &quot;steel&quot;, and that tells you which will affect the Fair Folk.<br /> <br /> Real-world science tries to be mindful of the distinction between the map and the territory. This distinction can be applied as well to raksha, but the difference is that they are entities of the map, not of the territory. Just as focusing on the map in rather than the territory can lead you into trouble in the sciences, focusing on the territory rather than the map will lead you into trouble when dealing with the Fair Folk.</div> </div> </div> </div>This I can kinda get behind, but it relies on Old Realm = English a little bit much for my tastes.<br /> <br /> The other issue is that cast iron, pure iron and wrought iron all <i>really </i>suck as weapons. Cast iron because it would break the second you hit anything other than a fae (assuming it behaves like steel vs fae. If not it would break then too), pure iron because it's hard to cut things when you're as sharp as a butter knife at best, and wrought iron because the very impurities of slag that give it some structure are also exactly where it's gonna break after some use. So I guess you could argue that iron vs fae is effective only when it's not very effective otherwise but then what if I hit him with a steel pot strapped to a tree branch? Does that count?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cryo-Seraph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225326#post1225326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If we wanted the idea to get blended into Exalted more, maybe the thing needed in iron to do the job is not carbon for steel but instead white jade dust or something from the Imperial Mountain. If part of the idea of the Fae weakness is that most weapons don't do this damage, then something else should get designed in the system to handle it</div> </div> </div> </div>Alas, what you're describing here is actually the process to make jade weapons. Arms of the Chosen realizes that jade as stone is a bad choice to make weapons out of and specifically says that you add a jade powder to steel to create weapons and armor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":8804,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","user":"armyofwhispers","date":". 06-06-2018, 12:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:52:05-07:00","id":1225328,"title":"","content":"oh my god you're overthinking this dude","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5376-atg","name":"AtG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5376_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"691"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:53:25-07:00","id":1225329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AtG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225328#post1225328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">oh my god you're overthinking this dude</div> </div> </div> </div>Please don't threadcrap 'dude'","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:58:05-07:00","id":1225331,"title":"","content":"Well, &quot;modern&quot;. Witchcraft's been around since christianity.<br /> <br /> Alas, I'm failing to find the full sources and the people I usually reach for this are not responding. I'm out, all wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T09:58:37-07:00","id":1225332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225327#post1225327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This I can kinda get behind, but it relies on Old Realm = English a little bit much for my tastes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Me, too, which is why I added the EDIT: paragraph the same time you were writing.<br /> <br /> The way iron and the fae are set up in the 3e Core is not the way I'd have done things had I been the devs of that book, but given it is established, &quot;magical resonance with the power of names in Old Realm&quot; strikes me as a decent rationalization, in part because Old Realm is itself not a mundane language.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:08:29-07:00","id":1225337,"title":"","content":"I'm not a mythology expert, but my understanding is that the original idea of the various supernaturals of irish/celtic/what-have-you myth being vulnerable to &quot;iron&quot; didn't make any distinction between iron and steel, as there probably wasn't the sort of very detailed divide that we have for specific alloys now. Instead, the original myth was more likely making a distinction between &quot;modern&quot; iron weapons and the bronze weapons that preceded them. If you're trying to follow the inherent &quot;narrative&quot; of why iron should hurt raksha, as some people have suggested, this disparity between old and new is almost certainly the basis in real-world myth, not the exact percentage of carbon in the iron itself. With that in mind, I'd personally say that steel, like all iron based weapons, would count as aggravated.<br /> <br /> That said, I also consider most of Creation to be firmly bronze age territory, so most weapons are bronze.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2433-highpriest","name":"HighPriest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2433_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:09:32-07:00","id":1225339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225332#post1225332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Me, too, which is why I added the EDIT: paragraph the same time you were writing.<br /> <br /> The way iron and the fae are set up in the 3e Core is not the way I'd have done things had I been the devs of that book, but given it is established, &quot;magical resonance with the power of names in Old Realm&quot; strikes me as a decent rationalization, in part because Old Realm is itself not a mundane language.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah yes!<br /> <br /> and like I said, real life doesn't have to intrude on a fantasy setting. I do however, ask for internal consistency. Your explanation at least makes some amount of sense and I can sort live with that. Hell, given an actual explanation, you could set a difficulty on a sorcerous working that altered the status quo such that steel would be effective against fae in setting. You'd have to alter the very language of Old Realm to achieve it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:35:38-07:00","id":1225349,"title":"","content":"Honestly, Raksha being vulnerable to iron doesn't make much sense for Exalted mythos. Is a carry on from fair folk mythology, but it doesn't quite fit in Exalted.<br /> <br /> Elvenkind, as beings of the wild, are hurt by iron because it's simbolic of civilization. But Raksha aren't elves. They are beings of chaos.<br /> <br /> Raksha being vulnerable to jade (Since Jade represents order and stability) or silver (Luna's metal) would make a lot more sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12278,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12278-broken25","user":"Broken25","date":". 06-06-2018, 01:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12278-broken25","name":"Broken25","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:38:10-07:00","id":1225352,"title":"","content":"Ok, lets be real, if you took a fae, sat them down and asked why they're hurt by <i>this </i>form of iron with a certain carbon content hurt them, and <i>that</i> form of iron with even more carbon hurt them, but this <i>other</i> form of iron, with a carbon somewhere in the middle that people called steel <i>doesn't</i> the answer would probably be:<br /> <br /> &quot;Because shut up. Shut up is why.&quot;<br /> <br /> They're mad dreamfolk from outside reality. Why would they have a bane that <i>makes sense?</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:45:30-07:00","id":1225354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225352#post1225352\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, lets be real, if you took a fae, sat them down and asked why they're hurt by <i>this </i>form of iron with a certain carbon content hurt them, and <i>that</i> form of iron with even more carbon hurt them, but this <i>other</i> form of iron, with a carbon somewhere in the middle that people called steel <i>doesn't</i> the answer would probably be:<br /> <br /> &quot;Because shut up. Shut up is why.&quot;<br /> <br /> They're mad dreamfolk from outside reality. Why would they have a bane that <i>makes sense?</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Because it's something that every single one of them has. It's a narrative so pervasive that it's included in the being of every single one of the Raksha and has been forever. As beings of pure chaos, this is like the only thing that they <i>all</i> have in common.<br /> <br /> I just want it to make some kind of sense as to why this is the case. Sorry that &quot;Because shut up&quot; is a little bit too arbitrary and stupid for my suspension of disbelief to survive?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:50:10-07:00","id":1225357,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225354#post1225354\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because it's something that every single one of them has. It's a narrative so pervasive that it's included in the being of every single one of the Raksha and has been forever. As beings of pure chaos, this is like the only thing that they <i>all</i> have in common.<br /> <br /> I just want it to make some kind of sense as to why this is the case. Sorry that &quot;Because shut up&quot; is a little bit too arbitrary and stupid for my suspension of disbelief to survive?</div> </div> </div> </div>Iron hurts Raksha because iron hurts Eurofae, and nobody bothered to make an explanation that fits the Exalted mythology.<br /> <br /> That's literally it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12278-broken25","name":"Broken25","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T10:54:53-07:00","id":1225359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>YeOfLittleFaith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225285#post1225285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it's at all about the chemical purity of iron. At face value, the simple implication of the distinction made by the Iron bane in Ex3, is that it's defined by the practical and historical differences between the iron and steel industries. Culturally, different developments in the treatment and processing of iron gave rise to forms with different qualities and applications over time, and by common understanding <i>this </i>is the line where the game draws a disparity between Iron and Steel.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Steel has and has nearly always had a distinct cultural cachet not shared by wrought or cast iron, due to a combination of its superior properties and the large differences in the techniques and technologies required to make it vs. either of its neighbors. That sort of cultural distinction is in turn the sort of thing that could quite plausibly, in a setting such as Exalted's, result in a metaphysical difference in how it interacts with beings of chaos from outside Creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T11:09:10-07:00","id":1225363,"title":"","content":"Hmm, this thread has actually convinced me that it's a fair point, and not one that I can readily imagine an answer to.<br /> <br /> \u200bIt reminds me of Changeling: the Lost, which tried to reconcile the idea through the notion of a form of iron that had been worked without ever heating it, since it needed to deal with the idea of fae in a modern world in which products with iron content are quite ubiquitous.<br /> <br /> The original folklore doesn't seem to take place with an assumption that people are going to be attempting to fight ghosts, witches and fairies with actual weapons; just that if you didn't want them sneaking into your home at night, hang an old horseshoe from the door post, which probably had less to do with the material and more with being a thing that you owned with a distinctive shape and not much practical use.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225327#post1225327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Arms of the Chosen realizes that jade as stone is a bad choice to make weapons out of and specifically says that you add a jade powder to steel to create weapons and armor.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bArms of the Chosen realised nothing; the idea that jade weapons are made from a steel alloy goes back at least as far as the First Edition book Manacle and Coin, and possibly to the core.<br /> <br /> \u200bHeck, <i>none</i> of the magical materials are supposed to be used in pure form in the creation of weapons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T11:29:39-07:00","id":1225369,"title":"","content":"Assuming that most steel is used in weapons and armor in Exalted, that means that cast and wrought iron are used primarily (and exclusively?) for tools. Tools help people impose order on things. They build things that endure, unchanging. Tools are a symbol of order, the thing most inimical to Raksha. So, it makes sense for the materials most associated with tools and order to harm Raksha while steel, the material of death and destruction, does not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T11:44:23-07:00","id":1225382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225369#post1225369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Assuming that most steel is used in weapons and armor in Exalted, that means that cast and wrought iron are used primarily (and exclusively?) for tools.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's really not the case, however. Steel is a good material for many tools as well - anything you need both the ability to hold a sharp edge and decent resilience. A chisel, for example, is far better when made of steel than wrought or cast iron. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> steel, the material of death and destruction, .</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd disagree with this characterization as well, frankly. Historically, weapons have quite often been seen as symbols of order. This makes sense, because they were frequently used by those in authority to <i>enforce</i> that order on people. Consider, for example, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fasces\" target=\"_blank\">fasces</a>, used in Classical Rome as a major symbol of law and order, and incorporating an axe as a major feature of its design. Or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Justice\" target=\"_blank\">Lady Justice</a>, almost always depicted holding a sword.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T11:53:37-07:00","id":1225386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225382#post1225382\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's really not the case, however. Steel is a good material for many tools as well - anything you need both the ability to hold a sharp edge and decent resilience. A chisel, for example, is far better when made of steel than wrought or cast iron.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'd disagree with this characterization as well, frankly. Historically, weapons have quite often been seen as symbols of order. This makes sense, because they were frequently used by those in authority to <i>enforce</i> that order on people. Consider, for example, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fasces\" target=\"_blank\">fasces</a>, used in Classical Rome as a major symbol of law and order, and incorporating an axe as a major feature of its design. Or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Justice\" target=\"_blank\">Lady Justice</a>, almost always depicted holding a sword.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> ...I mean...those are executionrs tools though...I agree, its probably unfair to say steel is &quot;more&quot; death than iron (Maybe more forged? More artifice compared to meteoric ore?) but those examples are in fact <i>death</i> related.<br /> A better counter point might be blacksmithing in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blacksmiths_of_western_Africa\" target=\"_blank\">West Africa</a>. There, blacksmiths were associted with leadership and sacred positions a well.<br /> <br /> I think the reason iron hurts fae is that a) the Fae are Eurofae, who were kept at bay by iron in the past (Note: wikipedia at least suggests that originally cold iron meant any bladed iron, including steel) and b) its a rare and unusal weapon material that allows a specialized kit (like peach wood and spirits).<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T11:59:52-07:00","id":1225390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225382#post1225382\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's really not the case, however. Steel is a good material for many tools as well - anything you need both the ability to hold a sharp edge and decent resilience. A chisel, for example, is far better when made of steel than wrought or cast iron.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why I qualified it, but I'm willing to bet most steel goes to tools of fighting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd disagree with this characterization as well, frankly. Historically, weapons have quite often been seen as symbols of order. This makes sense, because they were frequently used by those in authority to <i>enforce</i> that order on people. Consider, for example, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fasces\" target=\"_blank\">fasces</a>, used in Classical Rome as a major symbol of law and order, and incorporating an axe as a major feature of its design. Or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Justice\" target=\"_blank\">Lady Justice</a>, almost always depicted holding a sword. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but the Wyld understands violence. It understands death. It doesn't understand creating something unchanging and permanent. That is completely alien and opposite to it, which is why it is undone by it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:02:07-07:00","id":1225391,"title":"","content":"I\u2019d just say they\u2019re vulnerable to wrought iron and not steel because they encountered wrought iron wielded against them first, so that\u2019s what (to use CoD terminology) the bane formed around. It\u2019s not anything more inherent in the nature of iron than that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:05:18-07:00","id":1225394,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225354#post1225354\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because it's something that every single one of them has. It's a narrative so pervasive that it's included in the being of every single one of the Raksha and has been forever. As beings of pure chaos, this is like the only thing that they <i>all</i> have in common.<br /> <br /> I just want it to make some kind of sense as to why this is the case. Sorry that &quot;Because shut up&quot; is a little bit too arbitrary and stupid for my suspension of disbelief to survive?</div> </div> </div> </div>If Raksha vulnerability has any relationship to carbon content that would be too arbitrary and stupid for my suspension of disbelief to survive. They\u2019re weakness is narrative, not chemical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 06-06-2018, 03:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:07:13-07:00","id":1225395,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225386#post1225386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">those are executionrs tools though...I agree, its probably unfair to say steel is &quot;more&quot; death than iron (Maybe more forged? More artifice compared to meteoric ore?) but those examples are in fact <i>death</i> related.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but my point was, there's no distinction between &quot;death and destruction&quot; and &quot;order&quot;. A weapon can be a symbol of both, so it doesn't make any sense to suggest, as the poster I was responding to did, that tools represent order while weapons don't. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:14:50-07:00","id":1225400,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225390#post1225390\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but the Wyld understands violence. It understands death. <b>It doesn't understand creating something unchanging and permanent</b>. That is completely alien and opposite to it, which is why it is undone by it.</div> </div> </div> </div>a) that would mean that they just don't understand this whole 'Creation' business and just kinda do their thing. This is explicitly not true. They hate creation and they would never make something like it themselves, but to say that they don't understand the creation of unchanging things in nonsense.<br /> <br /> b) iron is a terrible example of a material that represents 'unchanging and permanent' If you wanted that, you'd be better served going for White Jade or literally any of the MM. Or even normal rock. Metals are valuable precisely because they are maleable and formable. You can't forge something that is unchanging and permanent because that's literally the opposite of what you're doing. <br /> <br /> c) none of which explains why <i>iron </i>works and <i>steel</i> doesn't.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:18:10-07:00","id":1225401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225391#post1225391\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019d just say they\u2019re vulnerable to wrought iron and not steel because they encountered wrought iron wielded against them first, so that\u2019s what (to use CoD terminology) the bane formed around. It\u2019s not anything more inherent in the nature of iron than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>They fought against the primordials first.<br /> <br /> What you think that Malfeas smacked them in the face with an <i>iron </i>weapon and they became allergic to it? Leave WoD out of this, it's not terribly relevant. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225391#post1225391\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019d just say they\u2019re vulnerable to wrought iron and not steel because they encountered wrought iron wielded against them first, so that\u2019s what (to use CoD terminology) the bane formed around. It\u2019s not anything more inherent in the nature of iron than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why I asked the question in the OP. I'm looking for a setting-consistent justification of an extremely arbitrary and weird artifact of the 3E core.<br /> <br /> &quot;Just because&quot; is and always will be a bullshit answer. Sorry to burst your bubble.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:26:24-07:00","id":1225403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225401#post1225401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They fought against the primordials first.<br /> <br /> What you think that Malfeas smacked them in the face with an <i>iron </i>weapon and they became allergic to it? Leave WoD out of this, it's not terribly relevant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that WoD is relevant, Exalted was supposed to be a pre-pre-history WoD. And the fae vulnerabilty to iron in Exalted is related to fae vulnerability to iron in WoD, and whatever mythology WoD got it from.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225401#post1225401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is why I asked the question in the OP. I'm looking for a setting-consistent justification of an extremely arbitrary and weird artifact of the 3E core.<br /> <br /> &quot;Just because&quot; is and always will be a bullshit answer. Sorry to burst your bubble.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're setting yourself up for frustration by expecting a scientific method response to this. There isn't one.<br /> <br /> If something is named &quot;iron&quot; and is primarily believed to be &quot;iron&quot; it will work. If the iron turned to steel, it doesn't, just because. The chemical composition is irrelevant.<br /> <br /> Feel free to change it in your games to something that suits you better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:29:05-07:00","id":1225405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225400#post1225400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">a) that would mean that they just don't understand this whole 'Creation' business and just kinda do their thing. This is explicitly not true. They hate creation and they would never make something like it themselves, but to say that they don't understand the creation of unchanging things in nonsense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? Name one thing a Raksha has created that is permanent and everlasting?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> b) iron is a terrible example of a material that represents 'unchanging and permanent' If you wanted that, you'd be better served going for White Jade or literally any of the MM. Or even normal rock. Metals are valuable precisely because they are maleable and formable. You can't forge something that is unchanging and permanent because that's literally the opposite of what you're doing. </div> </div> </div>Read further up. I said iron was a SYMBOL of the act of creation and bringing order. Steel is used in more varied ways and is not as good of a symbol.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> c) none of which explains why <i>iron </i>works and <i>steel</i> doesn't. </div> </div> </div>Because symbolism and narrative matters to the Fae, and iron is a far stronger symbol of order than steel is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8719,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","user":"Florin","date":". 06-06-2018, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:42:32-07:00","id":1225412,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225405#post1225405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Really? Name one thing a Raksha has created that is permanent and everlasting?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>In what way do the Fae not understand what permanence is despite never having created it themselves? They hate creation and everything it stands for (or at least some of them do). This doesn't mean that they cannot comprehend the concept of permanence. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225405#post1225405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Read further up. I said iron was a SYMBOL of the act of creation and bringing order. Steel is used in more varied ways and is not as good of a symbol.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wait, go back. Suddenly you're saying that iron is a symbol of order while steel is not? Before you werer saying that iron was a symbol of unchanging and permanence. These seem to be mutually exclusive. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225405#post1225405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because symbolism and narrative matters to the Fae, and iron is a far stronger symbol of order than steel is.</div> </div> </div> </div>How? You keep making these claims with no evidence whatsoever backing them up. If you have some reason to believe this, please explain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:44:45-07:00","id":1225414,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225401#post1225401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They fought against the primordials first.<br /> <br /> What you think that Malfeas smacked them in the face with an <i>iron </i>weapon and they became allergic to it? Leave WoD out of this, it's not terribly relevant.<br /> <br /> <br /> Which is why I asked the question in the OP. I'm looking for a setting-consistent justification of an extremely arbitrary and weird artifact of the 3E core.<br /> <br /> &quot;Just because&quot; is and always will be a bullshit answer. Sorry to burst your bubble.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> so as penance for giving basically a bullshit answr, it might be irons association with the element of Earth and the Center (The Blessed Isle has huge amounts of iron, and such). I think another option might be (tying to the symbolism angle) is that early &quot;better&quot; weapons were of iron instead of bronze? But no, that doesnt make much sense either. I think the fruitful course here is to examine the meaning of iron in Creations history over the use of steel. It might be that with the advent of iron, a much more availble material and used in the cosmic omphalos, Creation was able to create large organized armies more durabl than before? And it's just age that makes it useful---given time steel will be as effective?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:46:30-07:00","id":1225417,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TGUEIROS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225403#post1225403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except that WoD is relevant, Exalted was supposed to be a pre-pre-history WoD. And the fae vulnerabilty to iron in Exalted is related to fae vulnerability to iron in WoD, and whatever mythology WoD got it from.</div> </div> </div> </div>This wasn't even that true in 1st edition and as a concept has been dead for more than a decade and two editions. Lunars are not werewolves, abyssals are not vampires, the raksha are not elves.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TGUEIROS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225403#post1225403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're setting yourself up for frustration by expecting a scientific method response to this. There isn't one.<br /> <br /> If something is named &quot;iron&quot; and is primarily believed to be &quot;iron&quot; it will work. If the iron turned to steel, it doesn't, just because. The chemical composition is irrelevant.<br /> <br /> Feel free to change it in your games to something that suits you better.</div> </div> </div> </div>If all you can do is claim that it works because magic, then please feel free to continue to do so in your own game and stop trying to make everyone believe as you do.<br /> <br /> I didn't ask for a scientific answer to this question. I asked for a setting-consistent answer and so far the only person who's actually come up with something was Versio.<br /> <br /> Edit: And now Epee102 as well. Thank you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:17:00-07:00","id":1225422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225412#post1225412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In what way do the Fae not understand what permanence is despite never having created it themselves? They hate creation and everything it stands for (or at least some of them do). This doesn't mean that they cannot comprehend the concept of permanence.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're creatures of chaos. Permanence and order is anathema to them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Wait, go back. Suddenly you're saying that iron is a symbol of order while steel is not? Before you werer saying that iron was a symbol of unchanging and permanence. These seem to be mutually exclusive. </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure saying iron is a symbol of order and then saying that iron is a symbol of unchanging and permanence isn't mutually exclusive. They're different ways of saying the same thing. Something constantly changing is not ordered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How? You keep making these claims with no evidence whatsoever backing them up. If you have some reason to believe this, please explain. </div> </div> </div>To quote myself, since you apparently didn't read back, as I suggested:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...cast and wrought iron are used primarily (and exclusively?) for tools. Tools help people impose order to things. They build things that endure, unchanging. Tools are a symbol of order, the thing most inimical to Raksha. </div> </div> </div>Steel isn't as good as a symbol because while it is used it constructive tools, it's also associated with death, murder, etc. And since death is often used narratively as a symbol for change and transition, that weakens the use of steel as a symbol for order.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:26:05-07:00","id":1225424,"title":"","content":"When the fae legends were mostly told, steel and iron were quite different in common view. Steel required special ore from far away places, master smiths to even smelt and was used primarily to make things lighter than iron or bronze. As in, steel swords could be thinner and still not break. Steel armour could be lighter and still hold up to blows.<br /> <br /> Iron, meanwhile, continued to be used for manacles, cages and balls and chains, where they were pretty much not replaced by steel until the legends of fae creatures had passed into mostly obscurity. In the real world, this was done due to cost.<br /> <br /> But in Exalted it means that, thematically, iron is heavier, and more commonly associated with forcing order than steel is. It is a narratively weightier substance than flashy steel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I thank the Devs for the great game of Exalted!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12735-bjornthefellhanded","name":"BjornTheFellhanded","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12735_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"327"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:27:11-07:00","id":1225426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Florin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225422#post1225422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Steel isn't as good as a symbol because while it is used it constructive tools, it's also associated with death, murder, etc. And since death is often used narratively as a symbol for change and transition, that weakens the use of steel as a symbol for order.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just because you want something to be a symbol of death doesn't make it so. The VAST majority of the tools we use are steel. <br /> <br /> Nobody makes cast iron hammers. Where do you get this idea that this was ever not the case? Just because you can make a cast iron frying pan doesn't mean you'd have a cast iron knife.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:28:45-07:00","id":1225428,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BjornTheFellhanded</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225424#post1225424\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When the fae legends were mostly told, steel and iron were quite different in common view.</div> </div> </div> </div>No they were not. This is a common misconception that arose in the 20th Century. cold iron = steel = cold iron.<br /> <br /> In fairy tales it hurt fae because it was high tech and they were all old world. This explanation does not hold up in Exalted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BjornTheFellhanded</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225424#post1225424\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Iron, meanwhile, continued to be used for manacles, cages and balls and chains, where they were pretty much not replaced by steel until the legends of fae creatures had passed into mostly obscurity. In the real world, this was done due to cost.</div> </div> </div> </div>No it wasn't. Manacles were steel. Because making a manacle you could break with a hard strike wasn't the most effective thing ever.<br /> <br /> Chains were never made of 'iron'. Seriously, where do you guys get this idea? Iron in pure state is too soft and cast iron is so brittle as to be easy to break with a directed, sharp strike. <br /> <br /> You'd have to make chains the thickness of your arm to make them out of cast iron. Nobody would waste that much iron.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":8804,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","user":"armyofwhispers","date":". 06-06-2018, 04:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:38:27-07:00","id":1225433,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225401#post1225401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you think that Malfeas smacked them in the face with an <i>iron </i>weapon</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bHmm, there's a thought... <br /> <br /> \u200bI can't get to my book at the moment, can you remind me if Octavian's weapon is made from iron or lead?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:42:56-07:00","id":1225436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225417#post1225417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This wasn't even that true in 1st edition and as a concept has been dead for more than a decade and two editions. Lunars are not werewolves, abyssals are not vampires, the raksha are not elves.<br /> <br /> If all you can do is claim that it works because magic, then please feel free to continue to do so in your own game and stop trying to make everyone believe as you do.<br /> <br /> I didn't ask for a scientific answer to this question. I asked for a setting-consistent answer and so far the only person who's actually come up with something was Versio.<br /> <br /> Edit: And now Epee102 as well. Thank you.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It is plenty setting consistent to say that it is because it is, why don't you realize that?<br /> <br /> You can want all the contrived semi scientific and semi logic metaphysical drivel you want, and it will be just as setting-consistent ad mine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:43:18-07:00","id":1225437,"title":"","content":"I think there's a type of iron you're forgetting about here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many items, before they came to be made of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mild_steel\" target=\"_blank\">mild steel</a>, were produced from wrought iron, including <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rivets\" target=\"_blank\">rivets</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nail_(fastener)\" target=\"_blank\">nails</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wire\" target=\"_blank\">wire</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Link_chain\" target=\"_blank\">chains</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Track_(rail_transport)\" target=\"_blank\">rails</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railway_coupling\" target=\"_blank\">railway couplings</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_pipe\" target=\"_blank\">water</a> and steam pipes, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(hardware)\" target=\"_blank\">nuts</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Screw\" target=\"_blank\">bolts</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horseshoe\" target=\"_blank\">horseshoes</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handrail\" target=\"_blank\">handrails</a>, wagon tires, straps for timber <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roof_truss\" target=\"_blank\">roof trusses</a>, and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ornamental_ironwork\" target=\"_blank\">ornamental ironwork</a>, among many other things.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:48:07-07:00","id":1225439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225433#post1225433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bHmm, there's a thought...<br /> <br /> \u200bI can't get to my book at the moment, can you remind me if Octavian's weapon is made from iron or lead?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Ex3 doesn't expressly says so, it calls his staff Equitable Resolution. He does have a defensive Parry based charm called Invincible Iron Bastion though, if that counts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:48:11-07:00","id":1225440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225428#post1225428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No it wasn't. Manacles were steel. Because making a manacle you could break with a hard strike wasn't the most effective thing ever.<br /> <br /> Chains were never made of 'iron'. Seriously, where do you guys get this idea? Iron in pure state is too soft and cast iron is so brittle as to be easy to break with a directed, sharp strike.<br /> <br /> You'd have to make chains the thickness of your arm to make them out of cast iron. Nobody would waste that much iron.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there's a type of iron you're forgetting about here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many items, before they came to be made of mild steel, were produced from wrought iron, including rivets, nails, wire, chains, rails, railway couplings, water and steam pipes, nuts, bolts, horseshoes, handrails, wagon tires, straps for timber roof trusses, and ornamental ironwork, among many other things.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:51:29-07:00","id":1225443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225433#post1225433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bHmm, there's a thought...<br /> <br /> \u200bI can't get to my book at the moment, can you remind me if Octavian's weapon is made from iron or lead?</div> </div> </div> </div>Alas, the 3E book makes mention of Equitable Resolution but does not describe it or the materials it was made of. <br /> <br /> Although if we're going the route of the raksha being vulnerable to iron due to actions of primordials before the big war, we could always speculate that there was a primordial who's 'thing' was iron (but not steel for some reason) and that he cursed the raksha. He may have died during the war or not, but the curse lives on. <br /> <br /> It sounds like a feat of solar circle sorcery to achieve that kind of thing, so I'd peg it as more of a primordial or perhaps a 3rd circle soul type thing to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:51:50-07:00","id":1225445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225428#post1225428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No they were not. This is a common misconception that arose in the 20th Century. cold iron = steel = cold iron.<br /> <br /> In fairy tales it hurt fae because it was high tech and they were all old world. This explanation does not hold up in Exalted.<br /> <br /> <br /> No it wasn't. Manacles were steel. Because making a manacle you could break with a hard strike wasn't the most effective thing ever.<br /> <br /> Chains were never made of 'iron'. Seriously, where do you guys get this idea? Iron in pure state is too soft and cast iron is so brittle as to be easy to break with a directed, sharp strike.<br /> <br /> You'd have to make chains the thickness of your arm to make them out of cast iron. Nobody would waste that much iron.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> A point of confusion might be that the Iron Age is when steel was masproduced, not iron.(again going off the wiki)<br /> In fairy tales, iron's cause of hurt isn't clear and is often ...well, dodgy. <br /> Metoric Iron might be a good examination, given some tibetan traditions. <span style=\"font-size:13px\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thokcha<br /> <br /> <br /> Hmmmm, but that asks why don't magic metals do aggravated damage? Alright, lets leave some symbolism aside. Iron might work because its unaltered metal from the core of the omphalos. Why iron instead of copper tho? Or tin? Hm. Perhaps different metals have alignments to elements, like they do in astrology and alchemy? Iron with Mars and Earth, maybe?</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:52:55-07:00","id":1225446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225443#post1225443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alas, the 3E book makes mention of Equitable Resolution but does not describe it or the materials it was made of.<br /> <br /> Although if we're going the route of the raksha being vulnerable to iron due to actions of primordials before the big war, we could always speculate that there was a primordial who's 'thing' was iron (but not steel for some reason) and that he cursed the raksha. He may have died during the war or not, but the curse lives on.<br /> <br /> It sounds like a feat of solar circle sorcery to achieve that kind of thing, so I'd peg it as more of a primordial or perhaps a 3rd circle soul type thing to do.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Autobot? Or Mardukth, what with him being an unworked stone?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:56:15-07:00","id":1225449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sith_Happens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225440#post1225440\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think there's a type of iron you're forgetting about here.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Which also makes no thematic sense because wrought iron was literally just a shittier version of steel! Pretty much every use of wrought iron was replaced with steel because it was the same application portfolio but superior!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T14:00:11-07:00","id":1225452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225446#post1225446\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Autobot? Or Mardukth, what with him being an unworked stone?</div> </div> </div> </div>Mardukth could fit, though that kinda leaves out why cast iron works.<br /> <br /> Autobot I'd say wouldn't fit. If any primordial was a metallurgist it was Mr. Tool Use himself. He's made of 'brass and steel' after all<br /> <br /> Edit: Actually that presents an interesting opportunity. Perhaps one of the primordials of old wanted to both f-up the raksha and simultaneously give the finger to Autobot. What better way than specifically excluding the middle ground that Autobot is made of when crafting a working to give the Raksha a weakness?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T14:02:15-07:00","id":1225453,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225426#post1225426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just because you want something to be a symbol of death doesn't make it so.</div> </div> </div> </div>I said it was associated with death as well as construction, not a symbol of death. You literally quoted those words.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The VAST majority of the tools we use are steel. </div> </div> </div>That we use now, yes. That we used back in the medieval times, no.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wikipedia</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Many items, before they came to be made of </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mild_steel\" target=\"_blank\">mild steel</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, were produced from wrought iron, including </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rivets\" target=\"_blank\">rivets</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nail_(fastener)\" target=\"_blank\">nails</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wire\" target=\"_blank\">wire</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Link_chain\" target=\"_blank\">chains</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Track_(rail_transport)\" target=\"_blank\">rails</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railway_coupling\" target=\"_blank\">railway couplings</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_pipe\" target=\"_blank\">water</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> and steam pipes, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(hardware)\" target=\"_blank\">nuts</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Screw\" target=\"_blank\">bolts</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horseshoe\" target=\"_blank\">horseshoes</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handrail\" target=\"_blank\">handrails</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, wagon tires, straps for timber </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roof_truss\" target=\"_blank\">roof trusses</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, and </span></span></span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ornamental_ironwork\" target=\"_blank\">ornamental ironwork</a><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">, among many other things</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> (And mild steel started being used in 1850.)<br /> <br /> So, we were using wrought iron well into the advent of railroads. Notice there are no weapons among those? That's part of my point.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nobody makes cast iron hammers. Where do you get this idea that this was ever not the case? </div> </div> </div>Apparently it came from your brain, because I never said it. If you're going to keep mis-representing what I say then we're done.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T14:15:24-07:00","id":1225458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225443#post1225443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Although if we're going the route of the raksha being vulnerable to iron due to actions of primordials before the big war, we could always speculate that there was a primordial who's 'thing' was iron (but not steel for some reason) and that he cursed the raksha. He may have died during the war or not, but the curse lives on.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI would think of it more in mythic terms, imagining one or more Primordials being described as wielding weapons of iron because I find the term and the image to be very evocative, with an idea that such wounds would bite deep into the raksha.<br /> <br /> \u200bStill, even that is mostly in terms of a fanciful explanation that the raksha themselves might offer. I agree on wanting a sense of clarity about what actually counts as iron, but I'm personally satisfied with iron being a fae weakness for the out-of-character familiarity, while the in-character explanation is largely a matter of speculation and mostly viewed as a gift horse whose mouth is not worth examining.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T15:19:57-07:00","id":1225481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225339#post1225339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">and like I said, real life doesn't have to intrude on a fantasy setting. I do however, ask for internal consistency. Your explanation at least makes some amount of sense and I can sort live with that. Hell, given an actual explanation, you could set a difficulty on a sorcerous working that altered the status quo such that steel would be effective against fae in setting. You'd have to alter the very language of Old Realm to achieve it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, plausibly what we could have here is a <i>pair</i> of workings, one of which made Fair Folk vulnerable to iron in a broad sense, then a counter-working to deliberately narrow the meaning of &quot;iron&quot; in Old Realm by some lunatic who was pro-Fae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T18:01:50-07:00","id":1225495,"title":"","content":"Hmmm... I could imagine that, at some point early on, an Eclipse extracted an oath from the Fae as a whole that would give them vulnerability to a common substance that many of their mundane weapons were made from and the Fae managed a bit of linguistic trickery that made their vulnerability into \u201cthat material specifically called iron\u201d instead of \u201cany material that contains iron.\u201d<br /> <br /> Alternately, iron\u2019s symbolic association with the stability of Creation allows it to be used in minor hedge magic to ward against and hurt fae. So any \u201ccold iron sword\u201d is going to have had a minor ritual performed on it that lets it do agg damage to Fae and may have any degree of carbon/iron content in the blade. Said rituals would be local things devised by most people who live near the Wyld.<br /> <br /> Well, or the iron needs to be as pure as possible so only wrought iron will do, not cast iron.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T18:58:47-07:00","id":1225502,"title":"","content":"So i have noticed one other aspect of iron vs. steel not being discussed as a possible reason: rust. While its been ages since i did chemistry stuff, modern assumptions of iron vs steel is how quickly or badly iron rusts first. That's why everyone loves stainless steel, rust proof, right?<br /> <br /> So maybe the weakness of iron via rusting is what harms Fae. Not only is Iron very 'orderly', but also represents the slow degradation and decay of all things, even metal. The calcification of Fae in Creation away from their food sources mirrors the idea some. So the better the steel, the less it rusts, the less itcan do to Fae. (Realistically, gradients to how much is needed to shift from Lethal to Agg would be tough to figure out and could go through trial and error, butwith game mechanics, it either is yes or no on Aggravated damage boost<br /> <br /> It also harkens to an idea that the Underworld aspects of iron and rust (who doesn't imagine Autocthon coughing out red rust as he speaks?) might be the real source of anti-Wyld effect.<br /> <br /> We could expand on the idea, anti Wyld cues mixing in rust to a tonic, using rust to stain a doorway to ward off Fae, etc","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T21:55:35-07:00","id":1225520,"title":"","content":"Personally, I just transferred the weakness to Jade* as I felt that made way more sense in Creation. So a chieftain of a tribe near the wyld borders might have a jade Charm on his house to keep creatures from the Wyld away. The majority who can't get their hands on Jade make do with lesser Charms and superstitions. Hopefully they find a Thaumaturge with the gift of keeping Raksha at bay...<br /> <br /> <br /> *<span style=\"font-size:10px\">And to a lesser extent pretty much any stone or metal taken directly from the Imperial Mountain. To a further lesser extent, metals and stones taken from Earth Demesnes, with those taken from minor Demesnes often loosing effect relatively quickly after being taken from the site.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T03:18:57-07:00","id":1225537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225449#post1225449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which also makes no thematic sense because wrought iron was literally just a shittier version of steel! Pretty much every use of wrought iron was replaced with steel because it was the same application portfolio but superior!</div> </div> </div> </div>We are talking about myths told before even alchemy was much of a science. I don't think chemical composition really factors into matters all that much.<br /> <br /> During the dark ages in Europe, iron could be forged by most smiths in a coal fired forge (that alone would make almost any forged iron have a carbon content that would chemically speaking make it steel). But that wasn't enough to make what was called 'steel' at the time. Steel required higher than usual grade ore and crucible forging, as a mere coal fire wasn't hot enough to purify that ore sufficiently. I believe that's also where the term 'cold iron' comes form: it was smelted at a lower temperature than the superior steels.<br /> <br /> Chemically speaking, most types of iron were actually low grade steel, sure. But there is a different definition based on the practicalities involved.<br /> <br /> But if that's not enough: Iron literally is the difference between wild and tame animals.Wild horses didn't have horseshoes. Iron shoes mark a horse as domesticated and a tool or pet of civilisation.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I thank the Devs for the great game of Exalted!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12735-bjornthefellhanded","name":"BjornTheFellhanded","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12735_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"327"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T05:44:14-07:00","id":1225551,"title":"","content":"Look...<br /> <br /> Iron is metaphysically associated with Earth essence, harmful to the Raksha. The more you work it/alloy it/whatever, the less useful it will be against the Raksha.<br /> <br /> Also? Iron weapons are SUPPOSED to be shitty! Lots of old Raksha charms have things like &quot;mortals get a bonus to resisting this if in contact with cold iron.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's not just about stabby times, it's about enabling Creation to feel more real by giving it superstitions. This guy who lives south of Winter Folk territory wears a horseshoe around his neck. This lady who lives near where the Lion Folk live wears an iron necklace when she goes out at night. This Guildsman who trades with the Raksha wears an iron coin in his shoe at all times to protect him from illusions and glamors.<br /> <br /> Weapons made from iron are probably ceremonial as often as not, and while they're technically usable against the Raksha, let's be real-a mortal's not gonna win that fight. It's mostly just there so the Raksha think twice about fucking with you, because if you get a hit in it's going that wound is going to have to become a long-term part of their story. Iron is there to remind the Fair Folk that you're real and can impose reality upon them.<br /> <br /> Steel just feels more symbolically mundane-it's less raw, less earthy, more ubiquitous, more useful, less esoteric. A magic weakness-metal should be something inconvenient to use and carry around, otherwise it'd be ubiquitous and feel less magic and interesting!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T11:43:05-07:00","id":1225641,"title":"","content":"The answer is literally, &quot;because narrative,&quot; the thing raksha are bound to. They are hurt by iron and not steel because that's how the story works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>SWTOR Referal: <a href=\"http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy</a>","edited":{"userid":414,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","user":"Gallus","date":". 06-07-2018, 02:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","name":"Gallus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"886"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T12:06:08-07:00","id":1225650,"title":"","content":"Roleplaying game settings are not reality simulators. Justify for, not against.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Incentive is not permission or justification.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/255-paranoiacombo","name":"ParanoiaCombo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar255_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"526"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T12:19:40-07:00","id":1225655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225551#post1225551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Look...<br /> <br /> Iron is metaphysically associated with Earth essence, harmful to the Raksha. The more you work it/alloy it/whatever, the less useful it will be against the Raksha.<br /> <br /> Also? Iron weapons are SUPPOSED to be shitty! Lots of old Raksha charms have things like &amp;quot;mortals get a bonus to resisting this if in contact with cold iron.&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> Steel just feels more symbolically mundane-it's less raw, less earthy, more ubiquitous, more useful, less esoteric. A magic weakness-metal should be something inconvenient to use and carry around, otherwise it'd be ubiquitous and feel less magic and interesting!</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I\u2019m not even sure creations iron/steel is the same as ours. Our carbon steel is stronger because carbon atoms introduce themselves into the iron crystal matrix and provide barriers against slip within the matrix. I\u2019m 99% sure that\u2019s not the case in Exalted. There\u2019s no microstructure analysis there.<br /> <br /> What you have instead is somebody working fire essence into the iron through coke ovens and just plain fire. It makes the metal more workable, sharper and biting like a flame, but if you can make it in one single casting and work it cold, or whatever, it retains much more of the stabilizing earth essence purity. <br /> <br /> Also as for why aggravated matters in combat. It doesn\u2019t, but it\u2019s a hell of an intimidation factor in the pre-combat social interaction when they look down and see your entire group is sporting weapons that make their very essence burn at a touch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T12:28:13-07:00","id":1225660,"title":"","content":"I don't really find many of these arguments convincing, I'm afraid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225551#post1225551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Iron is metaphysically associated with Earth essence, harmful to the Raksha. The more you work it/alloy it/whatever, the less useful it will be against the Raksha.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem here is that the iron that <i>does</i> harm the Raksha (wrought iron and cast iron) is not notably <i>less</i> worked than steel. The process of making wrought iron involves considerable hammering to work out most of the impurities (its name literally <i>means</i> &quot;worked iron&quot;, remember), while cast iron involves melting the iron down completely and pouring it into moulds. I'd argue that such a major transformation in the process of creating it is going to be perceived as more &quot;worked&quot; than steel. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not just about stabby times, it's about enabling Creation to feel more real by giving it superstitions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see how making the effects consistent with real-world superstitions is somehow making it impossible for Creationers to have superstitions? Remember, in the real world, the superstitions about iron affecting the supernatural didn't make a distinction between iron and steel. The peasant burying a knife under her door to ward off witches was not being careful to use only a <i>crappy</i> knife, made of wrought iron instead of steel. Rather, I suspect a good knife would probably have been seen as <i>better</i>, not worse. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Steel just feels more symbolically mundane-it's less raw, less earthy, more ubiquitous, more useful, less esoteric. </div> </div> </div> </div>Almost none of this is actually true compared to the materials that <i>do </i>harm Raksha, however. I don't believe that steel is &quot;less raw&quot; or &quot;less earthy&quot; than cast iron. It's certainly <i>less</i> ubiquitous than either wrought or cast iron (the reason both of those techniques continued once good steel was created was that they were still cheaper and easier to produce than steel for a long time). It's not &quot;less esoteric&quot; at all - steel making was generally considered a trade secret, one frequently respected and held in high demand, with considerable &quot;industrial espionage&quot; that went on. <br /> <br /> The only thing I'd concede is &quot;more useful&quot;, but I don't see why that should make it <i>less</i> useful, magically. Narrative tends to work hand-in-glove with symbolism, and the best symbol for a weapon that's very effective against monsters is... a good weapon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A magic weakness-metal should be something inconvenient to use and carry around, otherwise it'd be ubiquitous and feel less magic and interesting!</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a magic weakness should be <i>rarer</i> than otherwise, certainly. But I feel steel in Creation fits this niche quite well, since they're at a tech level where it's harder to produce. As I mentioned upthread, I don't think letting steel weapons hurt Raksha really hurts the balance of the game much at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T12:48:13-07:00","id":1225667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cryo-Seraph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225502#post1225502\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So i have noticed one other aspect of iron vs. steel not being discussed as a possible reason: rust. While its been ages since i did chemistry stuff, modern assumptions of iron vs steel is how quickly or badly iron rusts first. That's why everyone loves stainless steel, rust proof, right?<br /> <br /> So maybe the weakness of iron via rusting is what harms Fae. Not only is Iron very 'orderly', but also represents the slow degradation and decay of all things, even metal. The calcification of Fae in Creation away from their food sources mirrors the idea some. So the better the steel, the less it rusts, the less itcan do to Fae. (Realistically, gradients to how much is needed to shift from Lethal to Agg would be tough to figure out and could go through trial and error, butwith game mechanics, it either is yes or no on Aggravated damage boost<br /> <br /> It also harkens to an idea that the Underworld aspects of iron and rust (who doesn't imagine Autocthon coughing out red rust as he speaks?) might be the real source of anti-Wyld effect.<br /> <br /> We could expand on the idea, anti Wyld cues mixing in rust to a tonic, using rust to stain a doorway to ward off Fae, etc</div> </div> </div> </div>Stainless steel is a very specific kind of steel. It's alloyed with other metals. 99% of steel will rust the same as iron in any form.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T12:56:20-07:00","id":1225672,"title":"","content":"Here's my advice: if you know enough about metallurgy to understand why the distinction between iron and steel is nonsensical, and if it bugs you enough that it's lessening your fun, then let raksha be affected by steel in precisely the same way as iron. <span style=\"font-size:11px\">(&quot;But Kukla, how did they possibly pose a threat to the first age then, when a material that weakens them and turns off some of their cheap tricks was plentiful and common?&quot; &quot;I assume there were several orders of magnitude more of them at the time&quot;)</span><br /> <br /> This is all a work of fiction. Whatever holds you back, cut it away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T13:00:23-07:00","id":1225677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kukla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225672#post1225672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's my advice: if you know enough about metallurgy to understand why the distinction between iron and steel is nonsensical, and if it bugs you enough that it's lessening your fun, then let raksha be affected by steel in precisely the same way as iron. <span style=\"font-size:11px\">(&quot;But Kukla, how did they possibly pose a threat to the first age then, when a material that weakens them and turns off some of their cheap tricks was plentiful and common?&quot; &quot;I assume there were several orders of magnitude more of them at the time&quot;)</span><br /> <br /> This is all a work of fiction. Whatever holds you back, cut it away.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Also, people are trying to apply some kind of real world logic to creatures representing pure chaos. Making perfect sense is not something Raksha usually do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T13:18:44-07:00","id":1225686,"title":"","content":"Okay at the risk of repeating myself (and in the hope I can avoid repeating myself <i>again</i>)<br /> <br /> The entire point of this thread was to generate ideas as to WHY THE RAKSHA ARE VULNERABLE TO IRON BUT NOT STEEL in a way that is a) setting consistent and b) makes sense <br /> <br /> If your answer is 'Stop trying to make sense of magic' or 'just ignore it' Then you have completely missed the point of the thread and you're not adding anything constructive to the discussion. Feel free to peruse ANY of the other threads on the forum.<br /> <br /> I will also point out that the idea that iron and steel are different because 'reasons' has also been argued extensively on this thread, and I personally would like to move past it now.<br /> <br /> Otherwise, please continue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T13:28:52-07:00","id":1225695,"title":"","content":"armyofwhispers, I don't understand what you are looking for then. I see you asking for a difference between iron and steel (that would explain their effects on the Raksha), but you counter any argument made as to why they are different with citing chemistry indicating that they are not.<br /> <br /> I think in order to discuss why they have different effects, we must accept that they are different, at least in the world of exalted. If they were the same, then yeah, Raksha would fear both. But they don't, so there must be a difference, even if chemistry says there isn't.<br /> <br /> So either iron and steel are different, or the words don't mean the same thing in exalted as they do in real world chemistry. Take your pick, but you I think you need to accept one of the two in order to get what you seem to want out of this discussion.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I thank the Devs for the great game of Exalted!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12735-bjornthefellhanded","name":"BjornTheFellhanded","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12735_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"327"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T13:51:23-07:00","id":1225707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225655#post1225655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, I\u2019m not even sure creations iron/steel is the same as ours. Our carbon steel is stronger because carbon atoms introduce themselves into the iron crystal matrix and provide barriers against slip within the matrix. I\u2019m 99% sure that\u2019s not the case in Exalted. There\u2019s no microstructure analysis there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're confusing 'refuses to waste wordcount explaining the nitty-gritty atomic-level interactions&quot; with &quot;It definitely doesn't work the same way as in the real world.&quot;<br /> <br /> We don't need to go into molecular level physics to explain that iron+charcoal = harder metal. They didn't need to for centuries of blacksmiths doing the work of turning iron ore into tools.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225655#post1225655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you have instead is somebody working fire essence into the iron through coke ovens and just plain fire. It makes the metal more workable, sharper and biting like a flame, but if you can make it in one single casting and work it cold, or whatever, it retains much more of the stabilizing earth essence purity.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry? Where are you getting this? Is this your personal interpretation written as canonical fact, or do you have some reference that this is how it works?<br /> <br /> Is wrought iron any less forged than steel? Can you not cast steel (that's what most anvils are made of, and certainly the best ones)? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225655#post1225655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also as for why aggravated matters in combat. It doesn\u2019t, but it\u2019s a hell of an intimidation factor in the pre-combat social interaction when they look down and see your entire group is sporting weapons that make their very essence burn at a touch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually one thing that hasn't been pointed out is that iron would upgrade bashing damage as well. It would be impossible to knock out a fair folk with iron.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T14:39:18-07:00","id":1225729,"title":"","content":"I kind of want to think that something that qualifies as iron, whether it's cast or wrought or pure, is harmful to Wyld creatures because it's kind of dull. That the appearance and structure and use of it is just kind of dull, and the chaotic Essence of a raksha reacts poorly to contact with it. Not an elaborate explanation of something onerous and important that iron represents or embodies, just it being something basic and simple and that hurting creatures personified by the Wyld.<br /> <br /> \u200bCould alloys that would generally be described as steel be differentiated by properties such as being more pliant or better able to hold a sharp edge?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T15:17:16-07:00","id":1225742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225729#post1225729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I kind of want to think that something that qualifies as iron, whether it's cast or wrought or pure, is harmful to Wyld creatures because it's kind of dull. That the appearance and structure and use of it is just kind of dull, and the chaotic Essence of a raksha reacts poorly to contact with it. Not an elaborate explanation of something onerous and important that iron represents or embodies, just it being something basic and simple and that hurting creatures personified by the Wyld.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this might be possible. I'm not actually sure how shiny you can get wrought iron. The slag impurities suggest less than steel? You can certainly shine up cast iron though. Most cast iron has rough finish only because the easiest way to cast something is to make a sand mold and the metal takes on the surface of the sand it sits against in the mold. Sand casting has been used for centuries and I'd expect it to be the most common way of casting something. Other mold materials are possible though. <br /> <br /> I think to make this work you'd have to fudge the finish properties of the materials. But then, if dull finishes hurt the raksha, why only iron with dull finishes :-/<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225729#post1225729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bCould alloys that would generally be described as steel be differentiated by properties such as being more pliant or better able to hold a sharp edge?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, sorta. Steel is most easily defined as being hard and strong enough for most useful applications while not being so hard as to behave in a brittle way. IE, it's hard enough for useful stuff but ductile enough to stretch and spring back. This is most likely how it's defined in creation terms since they're gonna have a hard time measuring the exact weight of carbon in the steel.<br /> <br /> The problem is that this describes wrought iron as well. The main difference there is that it's not as good at filling this niche. It's more brittle and more difficult to work than steel but still fits the more ductile than cast iron but harder than iron range.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T15:37:15-07:00","id":1225752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225742#post1225742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this might be possible. I'm not actually sure how shiny you can get wrought iron. The slag impurities suggest less than steel? You can certainly shine up cast iron though. Most cast iron has rough finish only because the easiest way to cast something is to make a sand mold and the metal takes on the surface of the sand it sits against in the mold. Sand casting has been used for centuries and I'd expect it to be the most common way of casting something. Other mold materials are possible though.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI didn't precisely mean dull as in the finish, but as a notion of it being kind of boring.<br /> <br /> \u200b... With maybe a bit of a reference to the finish. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">But then, if dull finishes hurt the raksha, why only iron with dull finishes :-/</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI have a certain sense of something that is alternately boring, and has the weight of mythos behind it; iron as a more evocative kind of boring than things that might be actually or mundanely boring. Something to both give a bit of weight behind the iron, and to inform why the Wyld receives it so; that in the typical obnoxious manner, it wants to have its cake and eat it too, so if there's going to be a mundane substance that is antithetical to it, it will only accept an impressive one.<br /> <br /> \u200bThat might be a bit paradoxical, but mythos can encompass paradox (and is arguably dependent upon it).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T16:33:54-07:00","id":1225763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225686#post1225686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay at the risk of repeating myself (and in the hope I can avoid repeating myself <i>again</i>)<br /> <br /> The entire point of this thread was to generate ideas as to WHY THE RAKSHA ARE VULNERABLE TO IRON BUT NOT STEEL in a way that is a) setting consistent and b) makes sense<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I'll take a stab at this (though I feel I'm repeating other people):<br /> <br /> It's not iron that's special, it's steel. Steel inspires people with the possibilities of what metallurgy can accomplish as the useless or limited utility forms of iron are transformed into one of the most potent technologies humanity has ever discovered. But the awe and wonder of what steel can do, means the Raksha can interact with it safely unlike other forms of iron. <br /> <br /> This is consistent with the setting, because of the dream-narrative nature of fae creatures. While steel isn't to the level of the standard magical materials, it could easily be called the first magical material that humans could access without thaumaturgy, sorcerery or those that naturally work Essence. Like the major magical materials, and the other minor special materials out there, their special standing in human imagination is strong enough that fae creatures aren't harmed directly by it.<br /> <br /> It makes sense because the idea of steel being 'special' in some fashion beyond chemical superiority is something we see in real world human mythology. Multiple cultures have myths about the steel produced in meteors, or the Greek concept of &quot;shining iron,&quot; so on. While steel was in the phase of becoming commonly understood as a thing to make, but still before mass production, it grew to have a special place in the minds of people. Even today people have illogical assumptions about how special Damascus steel and Japanese folded steel are (I mean, they look cool, but they're about turning mediocre steel into good steel, not good steel into awesome steel). In Creation there's no reason to assume there wasn't a similar psychological process. Steel is special, steel is what elevates metal working to a civilization growing tool.<br /> <br /> If that's not good enough... I'm not sure what any of us can really say.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T23:08:34-07:00","id":1225823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225707#post1225707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're confusing 'refuses to waste wordcount explaining the nitty-gritty atomic-level interactions&quot; with &quot;It definitely doesn't work the same way as in the real world.&quot;<br /> <br /> We don't need to go into molecular level physics to explain that iron+charcoal = harder metal. They didn't need to for centuries of blacksmiths doing the work of turning iron ore into tools.<br /> <br /> I'm sorry? Where are you getting this? Is this your personal interpretation written as canonical fact, or do you have some reference that this is how it works?<br /> <br /> Is wrought iron any less forged than steel? Can you not cast steel (that's what most anvils are made of, and certainly the best ones)?</div> </div> </div> </div>Erm so I wrote kind of a lot about why physics in Creation looks like physics in our world, but clearly isn't. Just skip the spoiler tags if you don't want to read it.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">There's a couple of things here that I'm going to break down, and the first is that physics in Creation, when you get down to it, are nothing like the physics of the real world. Upon a cursory look everything seems normal, which is good, we don't want to play in a fantasy universe where things are completely ridiculous strange and we constantly have to remember things like 25.6 hour days and stuff. Or maybe you do want to play in a universe like that but that's not Exalted.<br /> <br /> Objects are pulled downward, there's a sunrise and a sunset over the horizon, everything feels normal. The thing is, with our real world physics, it shouldn't, like at all.<br /> <br /> Sunset for somebody living in the east has the sunlight passing through tens of thousands of miles of air, air that includes water vapor and weather systems and smoke and all kinds of things. To somebody living in the East a sunset doesn't exist, the sun drops in the sky and then blooms out into a red haze long before it crosses the horizon. The same goes for sunrise in the West. So optics, clearly, is not the same.<br /> <br /> Gravity is another one. In our world things feel a downward force because massive objects attract each other through gravitational fields. In Creation that isn't how it works, or at least it almost certainly isn't. Everyone on the face of Creation feels a downward pull, which could theoretically be modeled by a big plane of mass that extends downward to a certain level. The problem here is the Wyld. This plane of mass would have to extend out into pure chaos at least as far as the distance between the border marches and the center without dissolving into something that doesn't generate gravity. Which considering gravity doesn't even exist in pure chaos would be hard.<br /> <br /> There's a bunch of other examples but I've gone on too long already. Germ theory is another quick one, apparently cauterizing wounds stops them from being infected rather than totally disabling the immune system in that area and making it a breeding ground for bacteria. Which are spread both on their own and by disease spirits.<br /> <br /> So at the very least electromagnetism and gravity don't work like they do in real life, and considering there's no atomic bombs in spite of Twilights with superhuman intelligence in the first age I'd maybe wager that there's no strong/weak nuclear force either. Which, if true, means that not a single fundamental force of our universe is duplicated in Exalted. So things definitely don't behave the same way in our world because they are fundamentally the same.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Okay so physics in Creation is not physics in our world, but it's not just pure magic-we-don't-have-to-explain-it either. Everything that's kind of magical has an essence explanation. In fact a lot of stuff that isn't magical has that even, like fireflies which are made by flies going into the Wyld and breeding with the concept of fire*. That little glow butt is a mote of fire essence.<br /> <br /> Raksha interact poorly with earth essence. The reason the Wyld doesn't push into Creation is because of the stabilizing Pole of Earth*, the Wyld-destroying hearthstones are earth aspected, in the DB charmset it's the earth charms, even in non-earth abilities, that beat back chaos. So what the Fey are weak to isn't iron exactly, they're weak to the earth essence in it. In fact the only other thing in the books that is so hostile to the Wyld is death essence, which I don't think cold worked iron has, I could be wrong.<br /> <br /> Let's build on this. Why does the process of making steel dilute the earth essence? It could be that it draws the earth essence out somehow, or burning it. Maybe putting it through the blast furnace suffuses it with a ton of fire essence, killing it's effectiveness with impurity. Maybe that's why it needs to be cold worked as well, any more fire than what was used to cast it in the first place is too much, becoming more a thing of fire than of earth by the end. A thing of the forge rather than the mine. So you cast it once right out of the purified ore and then work it with grindstones.<br /> <br /> Either way, somewhere in there is your answer. Or at least the easiest explanation that has a basis in setting physics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225707#post1225707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually one thing that hasn't been pointed out is that iron would upgrade bashing damage as well. It would be impossible to knock out a fair folk with iron.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ouch, that's a really good point. No mercy for sprites.<br /> <br /> <br /> *These are canon examples, not ones from my head.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T09:34:17-07:00","id":1225899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225823#post1225823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Erm so I wrote kind of a lot about why physics in Creation looks like physics in our world, but clearly isn't. Just skip the spoiler tags if you don't want to read it.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <di