mass would have to extend out into pure chaos at least as far as the distance between the border marches and the center without dissolving into something that doesn't generate gravity. Which considering gravity doesn't even exist in pure chaos would be hard.<br /> <br /> There's a bunch of other examples but I've gone on too long already. Germ theory is another quick one, apparently cauterizing wounds stops them from being infected rather than totally disabling the immune system in that area and making it a breeding ground for bacteria. Which are spread both on their own and by disease spirits.<br /> <br /> So at the very least electromagnetism and gravity don't work like they do in real life, and considering there's no atomic bombs in spite of Twilights with superhuman intelligence in the first age I'd maybe wager that there's no strong/weak nuclear force either. Which, if true, means that not a single fundamental force of our universe is duplicated in Exalted. So things definitely don't behave the same way in our world because they are fundamentally the same.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Okay so physics in Creation is not physics in our world, but it's not just pure magic-we-don't-have-to-explain-it either. Everything that's kind of magical has an essence explanation. In fact a lot of stuff that isn't magical has that even, like fireflies which are made by flies going into the Wyld and breeding with the concept of fire*. That little glow butt is a mote of fire essence.<br /> <br /> Raksha interact poorly with earth essence. The reason the Wyld doesn't push into Creation is because of the stabilizing Pole of Earth*, the Wyld-destroying hearthstones are earth aspected, in the DB charmset it's the earth charms, even in non-earth abilities, that beat back chaos. So what the Fey are weak to isn't iron exactly, they're weak to the earth essence in it. In fact the only other thing in the books that is so hostile to the Wyld is death essence, which I don't think cold worked iron has, I could be wrong.<br /> <br /> Let's build on this. Why does the process of making steel dilute the earth essence? It could be that it draws the earth essence out somehow, or burning it. Maybe putting it through the blast furnace suffuses it with a ton of fire essence, killing it's effectiveness with impurity. Maybe that's why it needs to be cold worked as well, any more fire than what was used to cast it in the first place is too much, becoming more a thing of fire than of earth by the end. A thing of the forge rather than the mine. So you cast it once right out of the purified ore and then work it with grindstones.<br /> <br /> Either way, somewhere in there is your answer. Or at least the easiest explanation that has a basis in setting physics.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Ouch, that's a really good point. No mercy for sprites.<br /> <br /> <br /> *These are canon examples, not ones from my head.</div> </div> </div> </div>First, thank you for with the topic rather than dismissing it. I appreciate everyone in this thread who's actually put some thought into this.<br /> <br /> Now, as for your post. I absolutely agree that physics has a different basis in Exalted. As you pointed out, gravity works differently, the day night cycle works differently, much of the scientific concepts that underpin our world are not the same as those that underpin creation. I also agree that creation is set up such that most things are simple to understand from our perspective. Things like gravity being the same everywhere in creation and everyone having the same day/night require different applications of fantasy to achieve. Due to this very forced similarity to Earth, I would argue that the majority of forging/metalworking techniques are the same as they were in Earth in the iron age. The reasons that a smith might hammer steel repeatedly might be different but I believe for the smith, it makes no difference whether he's forging something to properly work the crystal structure and balance the carbon content or if he's bashing the earth essence and molding the fire essence through it. I doubt he knows the specifics of it. It's not like they had microscopes in the iron age to look at crystal structures either.<br /> <br /> However, I still disagree with you. <br /> <br /> Primarily, I reject the idea that the weakness of the raksha has anything to do with Earth essence of any sort. <br /> <br /> First, it makes no sense that iron represents earth essence for two reasons: 1. Iron and metals are more the purview of Autochthon than Gaia. There might be a few elementals made of iron or other metals, but I think we can agree that the majority of them are made of earth or stone. However, elementals of metal and iron are common in the halls of Autochthonia. 2. both wrought iron and cast iron count against the weakness but steel does not. Wrought iron is often as worked as steel is, in the forge. Some more some less. An argument could be made that wrought iron includes some amount of the slag (Earth essence?) that would normally be removed for good steel. I would counter that cast iron does <i>not</i> include these impurities and that it requires good metal as well. It's easier to get decent cast iron out of shitty ore as you can run it through the coal more often and draw out the slag. This adds carbon to the cast iron and would make it useless for steel. So if the coal burning represents fire essence then cast iron would have more of it. Cast iron is also melted down and poured into a cast which requires a much higher heat than steel forging.<br /> <br /> Next, let's examine the far stronger representations of Earth essence: White Jade, The elemental Aura of Earth Aspect Dragon Bloods and earth elementals. None of these things trigger the weakness of the Fae. White jade in weapon form is apparently alloyed with steel. However, pure white jade (which is the magical material created from exposure to massive amounts of earth essence) doesn't work either. You hit a fae with a block of white jade and he gets bludgeoning damage, not aggravated. Once again, there's a counter argument where you could say that white jade is created from earth essence but isn't earth essence itself. Okay, but why then does iron count? What amount the DB? Why is there no special benefit from pouring out pure Earth essence. Perhaps they aren't dumping out actual Earth essence but Earth essence through a lens of mortality. I dunno, maybe, but that's not how it's described in the books so far. Regardless it still doesn't work since Earth Elementals, beings made of the same Earth essence that comes from the center of creation don't harm Raksha any more than normal.<br /> <br /> Finally, if iron is so powerful a symbol of Earth essence that it trumps jade, earth aspected DB <i>AND </i>earth elementals, why isn't it a major component of every artifact having anything to do with earth essence? I'll remind you that it's specifically steel that's used in artifact jade applications.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T09:58:13-07:00","id":1225907,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BjornTheFellhanded</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225695#post1225695\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">armyofwhispers, I don't understand what you are looking for then. I see you asking for a difference between iron and steel (that would explain their effects on the Raksha), but you counter any argument made as to why they are different with citing chemistry indicating that they are not.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, he doesn't. He counters any explanation of the difference that is <i>incoherent</i> with an explanation of why it's incoherent. He's willing to accept an explanation that's entirely magical/symbolical if the magic/symbolism is coherent.<br /> <br /> For example, he was okay with the explanation that a linguistic distinction between &quot;iron&quot; and &quot;steel&quot; in the supernatural language of Old Realm is why creatures of myth and story who innately speak Old Realm react differently to the materials. He's not jumping-up-and-down enthusiastic about it, but that's not a complaint about coherence, just the aesthetics of Old Realm adhering to English usage (or an approximation thereof).<br /> <br /> We have a Dev-defined situation where both ordinary wrought iron and ordinary cast iron are indeed &quot;iron&quot; for the raksha vulnerability but ordinary steel is not &quot;iron&quot; for the same purpose. Most of the explanations given in this thread fail to work simultaneously for both kinds of ordinary &quot;iron&quot;, which means they fail to coherently explain the Dev-defined situation. They often can be <i>made</i> coherent by abandoning ordinary cast iron as effective, or abandoning ordinary wrought iron as effective, or adding steel as effective, but &quot;abandon the Dev-defined situation&quot; is pretty much an obvious choice already, so explanations that <i>have to</i> implicitly include one of those to become coherent aren't adding anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T10:14:34-07:00","id":1225912,"title":"","content":"So a discussion on the subject came up a while back on a DnD forum.* A point that came up from a book of medicine by Pliny the Elder talked about various medical rituals involving bits of iron and circles and all that stuff implying that for whatever reason iron was considered a kind of antimagic. So a thing that came up in that entry spoke of people referring to magnetic iron as &quot;live iron&quot; and was believed to have mystical properties, even listing causing greater wounds. <br /> <br /> Well just spitballing, but what if that's the connection for Creation? That a cold iron weapon wasn't heated to keep it alive and some property of the living metal causes it respond negatively to Rhaksha? <br /> <br /> <br /> *Having problems finding the thread again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T10:38:32-07:00","id":1225923,"title":"","content":"For those who absolutely positively must science this, may I propose a quadratic relationship between carbon content and fae-murdering ability?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Current project: <a href=\"https://monsterambulance.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Monster Ambulance</a>, wherein you save monsters. In your ambulance.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2240-colin_fredericks","name":"Colin_Fredericks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2240_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T11:03:43-07:00","id":1225931,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference#post1225276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So reality aside, how does this work in Exalted?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My idea is that it is a visual thing.<br /> This might be silly, and feel free to reject it if you want to ^^<br /> \u200b<br /> When my mind conjures up an image of 'iron', it is an image of a black metal, while my image for 'steel' is a light grey metallic. Now this most probably isn't -correct- but the point I want to make is that the idea of something is not necessarily the same as the object or thing that it actually &quot;refers&quot; to in reality (or in Creation, in this case!).<br /> So the fair folk may know or recognize 'iron' as &quot;the black metal, the one that hurts us&quot; (mayhaps they got to know it as such in ages past for one reason or another) and visually distinct from other metals, including steel. And since iron turned to steel no longer falls under the [rakshas'] idea of 'iron', it doesn't/can not hurt them in the same way.*<br /> <br /> Of course this is based on the assumption that iron is visually distinct from steel.<br /> And also based on my understanding of Creation as completely alien to the fair folk, who struggle to understand how things work within 'reality', and are only able to grasp pieces through larger ideas, symbols and myth. Such things that are recurring, and relevant, enough to the human experience as to leave marks in the collective conciousness and be directly relatable and recognizeable in near any corner of the world.<br /> <br /> But if iron isn't in fact visually distinct from steel, well. It always can be, in your Creation, right? it could be the black or rainbow coloured metal that, when mixed with coal to make the stronger, less colourful and more useful to society; Steel, loses it's well known hue and with it its mythological status.<br /> <br /> *Kind of like how in a bugs bunny cartoon, you won't fall if you do not realise you are standing in the air.<br /> <br /> <br /> (Sorry if this idea has been sggested before! I only skimmed the previous pages...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1920-kim","name":"Kim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T11:13:23-07:00","id":1225932,"title":"","content":"Ferros, Lord of Iron, hates the fair folk because in the Early Days he was created to hate the fair folk, to be anathema to them with his very Essence as a bulwark against them by the Primordials.<br /> <br /> Ferrum, the Steel Father, however, came into being much later, and considers himself a deity of construction and war, and while he doesn't love the Fair Folk, also does not have the burning essence inside himself to destroy them as part of his nature.<br /> <br /> I feel that might be a bit more in theme for an Exalted explanation than &quot;real world science says my fantasy world is stupid.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5771-krata-lightblade","name":"Krata Lightblade","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5771_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T16:18:34-07:00","id":1225989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225686#post1225686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The entire point of this thread was to generate ideas as to WHY THE RAKSHA ARE VULNERABLE TO IRON BUT NOT STEEL in a way that is a) setting consistent and b) makes sense</div> </div> </div> </div>Ohhh! I totally missed that. Mea culpa!<br /> <br /> Okay, so pure iron is the most pure expression of earth essence, and earth essence is the most stable sort of essence, by far. The 'elemental pole of iron' is in fact just a solid pole of iron, miles thick!<br /> <br /> Likewise, any iron that is completely pure is part of the elemental pole of iron - regardless of location. It really is that simple! Iron is one of the few substances that the Wyld cannot corrode, not one bit - in fact, pure iron is more stable in the Wyld than in most of Creation, because Creation understands what to do with the stuff, has rules and purpose for interfacing with it, and the Wyld only perceives it as pure, conceptual stability.<br /> <br /> So when you boop a raksha with a lump of pure iron, you're boopin' em' with the heart-metal of the Shaped World. Just as Creation itself is a wound thrust in to the chaos, casting it back, the heart-metal of the Shaped World will wound and shatter the chaos of individual raksha, and their magics.<br /> <br /> But even the slightest oxidation or tempering of the iron breaks that magic trick. It's not heart-metal of the world anymore, just metal. Still great shit for cutting off raksha heads, but not quite the same as whupping em' with the axle of the world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T16:54:54-07:00","id":1225990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kukla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225989#post1225989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ohhh! I totally missed that. Mea culpa!<br /> <br /> Okay, so pure iron is the most pure expression of earth essence, and earth essence is the most stable sort of essence, by far. The 'elemental pole of iron' is in fact just a solid pole of iron, miles thick!</div> </div> </div> </div>Frankly, I think you're being sarcastic here, which is kind of rude. I don't think that asking for suggestions for explanations for setting details which aren't particularly consistent, on the face of them, is an unreasonable request? <br /> <br /> But, just in case you're proposing this seriously, I'll point out that it totally fails to provide any explanation for wrought or cast iron, both of which aren't &quot;pure iron&quot; any more than steel is.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T17:29:52-07:00","id":1225995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225899#post1225899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, it makes no sense that iron represents earth essence for two reasons: 1. Iron and metals are more the purview of Autochthon than Gaia. There might be a few elementals made of iron or other metals, but I think we can agree that the majority of them are made of earth or stone. However, elementals of metal and iron are common in the halls of Autochthonia.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm aware this isn't going to be completely compelling to the larger point, but this doesn't seem accurate to the setting.<br /> <br /> First, the Primordials don't have exclusive purviews... they don't even have purviews in this sense. They are tautological entities. Iron and other metals are part of Gaia because they're part of her world body. Autochthon has a greater percentage of iron and metals because he's more limited in his world body, not because Gaia is less associated with such things. The differences in things like elementals are going to be flavor. Gaia's metal elementals as part of the greater hierarchy of earth elementals, are going to be more attuned to the natural occurring forms of metals, because Gaia is the world as it exists before people do things to it. Autochthon's metal elementals are going to be more industrial as reflection of his nature as the Primordial of tools and inventions.<br /> <br /> Second, if we assume that the land that makes up Creation works like the crust of our planet, iron is one of the most important elements towards it's functioning, and the only one that's of any use at the technological level of the setting; that is, Creation isn't in a place where aluminum and silicon might be 'more' Earth essence than iron but it doesn't really matter without the ability to make use of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T20:01:19-07:00","id":1226011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225995#post1225995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm aware this isn't going to be completely compelling to the larger point, but this doesn't seem accurate to the setting.<br /> <br /> First, the Primordials don't have exclusive purviews... they don't even have purviews in this sense. They are tautological entities. Iron and other metals are part of Gaia because they're part of her world body.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gaia's world-body is not Creation; that's just a place she helped make. What her world-body might actually be, and whatever it might contain, is a mystery.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T22:50:46-07:00","id":1226020,"title":"","content":"Which is... why I didn't mention creation in that paragraph at all. I was comparing Gaia to Autochthon and why metal would be logically part of her nature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-08T23:27:54-07:00","id":1226027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225763#post1225763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'll take a stab at this (though I feel I'm repeating other people):<br /> <br /> It's not iron that's special, it's steel. Steel inspires people with the possibilities of what metallurgy can accomplish as the useless or limited utility forms of iron are transformed into one of the most potent technologies humanity has ever discovered. But the awe and wonder of what steel can do, means the Raksha can interact with it safely unlike other forms of iron.<br /> <br /> This is consistent with the setting, because of the dream-narrative nature of fae creatures. While steel isn't to the level of the standard magical materials, it could easily be called the first magical material that humans could access without thaumaturgy, sorcerery or those that naturally work Essence. Like the major magical materials, and the other minor special materials out there, their special standing in human imagination is strong enough that fae creatures aren't harmed directly by it.<br /> <br /> It makes sense because the idea of steel being 'special' in some fashion beyond chemical superiority is something we see in real world human mythology. Multiple cultures have myths about the steel produced in meteors, or the Greek concept of &quot;shining iron,&quot; so on. While steel was in the phase of becoming commonly understood as a thing to make, but still before mass production, it grew to have a special place in the minds of people. Even today people have illogical assumptions about how special Damascus steel and Japanese folded steel are (I mean, they look cool, but they're about turning mediocre steel into good steel, not good steel into awesome steel). In Creation there's no reason to assume there wasn't a similar psychological process. Steel is special, steel is what elevates metal working to a civilization growing tool.<br /> <br /> If that's not good enough... I'm not sure what any of us can really say.</div> </div> </div> </div>So this is one that I actually find interesting.<br /> I like it, a lot.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't really explain why every other base form of metals doesn't do what cold iron does.<br /> Is it the fact that steel stole every bit of faith humanity had in iron?<br /> If so, did bronze not do the same to copper?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/357-plotvitalnpc","name":"PlotVitalNPC","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar357_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T05:14:23-07:00","id":1226049,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Krata Lightblade</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225932#post1225932\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ferros, Lord of Iron, hates the fair folk because in the Early Days he was created to hate the fair folk, to be anathema to them with his very Essence as a bulwark against them by the Primordials.<br /> <br /> Ferrum, the Steel Father, however, came into being much later, and considers himself a deity of construction and war, and while he doesn't love the Fair Folk, also does not have the burning essence inside himself to destroy them as part of his nature.<br /> <br /> I feel that might be a bit more in theme for an Exalted explanation than &amp;quot;real world science says my fantasy world is stupid.&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>That both still begs the question of why iron in particular, and is not how gods work.<br /> <br /> It also continued to mischaracterize the original objection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T05:41:24-07:00","id":1226056,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PlotVitalNPC</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226027#post1226027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But it doesn't really explain why every other base form of metals doesn't do what cold iron does.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but I was trying to address the OP's initial and restated objection to the separation of &quot;iron&quot; and &quot;steel.&quot; The OP didn't ask why iron alloys at all.<br /> <br /> The &quot;why iron at all&quot; question isn't one that we can really answer to the OP's satisfaction. It is literally the way the setting was written because of a modern recontextualization of stray bits of myth and fantasy with no eye towards in-universe logic. Iron is anathema to fae creatures because WW did that with Changeling: the Dreaming first (because other RPGs did it before that), and it stuck. Any effort to explain that away is really back-filling it in.<br /> <br /> I think the &quot;Earth Essence&quot; argument is the best base argument, though needs to be expanded on to isolate iron specifically more effectively, and why you can use wrought or cast iron, instead of having to shape hematite to something useful to tie to the end of a stick and use that. However I don't think you can really make a functional version of this without some sort of &quot;a wizard did it,&quot; step; probably from the Primordials that wanted to have something their servants could use to attack the things of the Wyld that objected to what the Primordials were up to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T12:04:09-07:00","id":1226106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226020#post1226020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is... why I didn't mention creation in that paragraph at all. I was comparing Gaia to Autochthon and why metal would be logically part of her nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>We really can't make that conclusion for 3e. That the Elemental Dragons are her children and Luna's love for Gaia made the Lunars connected to animals are the only bits of canon that stand for this edition. Anything else is quite likely to be building off of abandoned canon. Gaia simply just isn't a focus for the setting anymore.<br /> <br /> As far as it matters in What Fire Has Wrought, metal is actually associated with Fire, too. Earth has stones, including various gems, but not metal. Further, fae don't seem to have a problem with Earth because it either turns them into Mountain Folk (pre- E:tFF) or is just another element that they can command (post recasting of the Mountain Folk as the Jadeborn).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 06-09-2018, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T12:14:16-07:00","id":1226109,"title":"","content":"What if we just replaced iron with some fictional metal which is found just as plentifully but which is good for nothing other than hurting fairies?<br /> <br /> It doesn't even have to be a metal; it could be a rock. The return of yellow jade!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T18:41:18-07:00","id":1226155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226106#post1226106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We really can't make that conclusion for 3e.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm perfectly willing to be contradicted by later 3e material when and if it happens. Until then, I'll use what information I have. The OP didn't say we have to stick solely to 3e sources to attempt to justify our reasoning, and I feel it's a safe assumption that Gaia and Autochthon are not changing significantly in theme in what they are, and what they &quot;do.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That the Elemental Dragons are her children... </div> </div> </div>Right, which includes all the Elemental Dragons of Earth, and all the metals that they would cover.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Gaia simply just isn't a focus for the setting anymore. </div> </div> </div>Gaia was never a major focus of the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T20:02:12-07:00","id":1226170,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226155#post1226155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm perfectly willing to be contradicted by later 3e material when and if it happens. Until then, I'll use what information I have. The OP didn't say we have to stick solely to 3e sources to attempt to justify our reasoning, and I feel it's a safe assumption that Gaia and Autochthon are not changing significantly in theme in what they are, and what they &quot;do.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, Autochthon did get kicked off the &quot;came up with Exaltation and made all of the Chosen's weapons in the Divine Revolution&quot; board, so we have reason to consider everything else less than reliably fixed for the time being.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Right, which includes all the Elemental Dragons of Earth, and all the metals that they would cover. </div> </div> </div>(Just the Elemental Dragon of Earth, surely)<br /> <br /> Earth has shifted in what it covers. To reiterate, Earth focused on stones, soil, and gems in WFHW, whereas Fire got the major association with metal. Fire might be the place to turn to if an Earth-based explanation doesn't pan out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Gaia was never a major focus of the setting. </div> </div> </div>2e sure seemed like it was giving it a go for a while, particularly with the Genesis Lords and xth souls of Gaia showing up everywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 06-09-2018, 11:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-09T22:22:20-07:00","id":1226201,"title":"","content":"In my game, back before the Primordial War, there were gods of Iron that totally hated the fair folk. They worked out how to make their Iron inimical to those from the Wyld. Later, in the First Age, Steel was created from Iron, and like all new things, got its own god. The god of steel, however, was a god of crafting and industry, and did not have any direct inimical feelings for the distant fey. Thus Iron hurts the Fair Folk, and steel does not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12847-kintani","name":"Kintani","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T09:46:12-07:00","id":1226256,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"center\">I like this dialogue, and Kintanis post.. Especially as the Fair Folk are about creating stories/dialogues and epic tales, so the Gods of Iron could have woven such a magnificent tale/adventure that all fae were bound to it's glory. Or such /shrug. </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13595-yawgrimas","name":"Yawgrimas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T13:41:52-07:00","id":1226290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference#post1225276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay so this is something that's bugged me in Exalted. In exalted the raksha are weak to weapons made of 'iron' but not weapons made of steel or other alloys. Apparently cast iron or wrought iron qualify as 'iron'<br /> <br /> So first, let's discuss real world definitions.<br /> <br /> pure iron: No alloy material. Not quite as bad as making a sword out of lead, but similar. Pure iron is a terrible material. It's soft, it's weak and it would be about as effective a weapon as a butter knife.<br /> <br /> Wrought iron: Extremely low carbon content by weight ~ 0.08% carbon. Also is alloyed with slag (up to 2% by weight). Slag is a byproduct of the transition from iron ore to iron. Better than pure iron, but only because the slag gives it some kind of structure. The carbon here provides some structure but not a whole lot.<br /> <br /> Steel: ~0.1% carbon by weight - 2.1% carbon by weight - Most people know what steel is. What most people don't realize is that 'steel' encompasses thousands of materials with distinct properties based on the exact carbon content and the heat treatment it undergoes. Some steel is also slightly altered by small amount of other alloying materials. In general, higher carbon = stiffer, harder steel while less carbon generally indicates softer steel but due to heat treatment you can get a some steels that invert this. All historical swords (and most other weapons in the iron age and beyond) that comprise iron of any type live in this range.<br /> <br /> Cast iron: 2.1%-4% carbon content by weight - Once again, multiple different types of cast iron with different properties. Pretty much the defining characteristic is that cast iron is hard and brittle. It does not behave as a ductile material. Once again, this range is terrible for making weapons out of. Here, though it's a matter of your sword shattering the first time you use it. This is the same problem as forging a sword out of steel, quenching it and then forgetting to temper it.<br /> <br /> So this is why I have trouble with the whole Raksha are weak to 'iron' but not 'steel' thing. Steel and cast iron are literally just iron+carbon with heat treatment and wrought iron throws in some extra slag for good measure. The range of steel that's closer to pure iron than wrought iron is huge. The range of steel that is closer to pure iron than cast iron is <i>all of it</i>.<br /> <br /> So reality aside, how does this work in Exalted? Because I'd love an explanation and I know too much about real world steel to just brush it aside (I had an entire engineering class dedicated to <i>just </i>steel and cast iron. I'm not a metallurgist, but I'm not a layperson either).</div> </div> </div> </div>It's <i>fucking magic</i> you pedantic <i>silly.</i> Stop overthinking it. There is no science involved. There are not chemicals or purities or any of this nonsense. It is <i>fucking magic.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/800-fenrir666","name":"Fenrir666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"300"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T14:44:27-07:00","id":1226301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fenrir666</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226290#post1226290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's <i>fucking magic</i> you pedantic <i>silly.</i> Stop overthinking it. There is no science involved. There are not chemicals or purities or any of this nonsense. It is <i>fucking magic.</i></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:red\">This is uncalled for. Please remember to keep your posts respectful of others, and of the conversation.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T18:55:57-07:00","id":1226348,"title":"","content":"What do people think salt water is in the setting?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T19:39:29-07:00","id":1226353,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226109#post1226109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What if we just replaced iron with some fictional metal which is found just as plentifully but which is good for nothing other than hurting fairies?</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel there's a very simple solution being overlooked, here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-10T19:46:57-07:00","id":1226354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226348#post1226348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do people think salt water is in the setting?</div> </div> </div> </div>Terrible tasting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T08:26:54-07:00","id":1226462,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226348#post1226348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do people think salt water is in the setting?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Little gods of salt skinny dipping in water?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13595-yawgrimas","name":"Yawgrimas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T08:34:39-07:00","id":1226466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yawgrimas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226462#post1226462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Little gods of salt skinny dipping in water?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it is obviously little gods of salt having orgies with litlle gods of water!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T08:35:01-07:00","id":1226467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226353#post1226353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel there's a very simple solution being overlooked, here.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bIt is straightforward, but I personally would find it unsatisfying for a couple of reasons.<br /> <br /> \u200bI was thinking about the manner in which folklore around the banes of fair folk has evolved over time, and considered how that might correspond to a scenario in the setting; in which raksha weaknesses can't be traced back to a single, iconic cause*, or particular interaction of Essence, but has been a gradual thing emerging from a number of factors that evolved over time. Some of them a matter of shapes or substances interacting, some a deliberate cultivation of narratives or sorceries in opposition to the fae, some a case of the Wyld Essence adapting to circumstances (in ways that might seem irrational to us, but make sense with an idea of a form of life and will that thrives in tumultuous circumstances).<br /> <br /> \u200bDrawing a bit directly on some of the folklore, I have a notion in which, in the very early days of the raksha preying on humans, it was an overall case of being repelled by the sight of little charms representing ingenuity and hard work and marking the sanctity of hearth and home, at a time when the raksha were more abstract, more feral, more dread figures in the night than David Bowie. Human society changed and the raksha changed to reflect them, and at a certain point the little doorpost charm lost its mythic weight, both in the significance that it held to people and the ease with which such things were manufactured. There were a few iterations of changing weaknesses, but it finally settled on the material from which some of those early charms were made; it still had qualities that would resonate with a weakness in the Fair Folk, while offset by being a bit less common and effective as something to make functional weapons from.<br /> <br /> \u200b*Much as the raksha themselves will distribute stories of such.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T08:39:50-07:00","id":1226468,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> I think Sunder was making a bad chemistry pun, rather than a speculative statement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T08:57:42-07:00","id":1226473,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saipjas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226468#post1226468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> I think Sunder was making a bad chemistry pun, rather than a speculative statement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I wish I was being that clever.<br /> <br /> But no, I just think that people are making this way more complicated than it needs to be. And I\u2019m not referring to the people whose suspension of disbelief is broken because they understand the realities of metallurgy.<br /> <br /> Why should we know why a particular substance is anathema to the Raksha? Why can\u2019t it be a mystery solved different ways at different tables?<br /> <br /> You can't provide an answer to the mystery that will satisfy everyone, and you shouldn\u2019t need to.<br /> <br /> But what you can do is substitute iron for a similarly useless metal that would be just as meaningful within the world of Exalted. A metal as useless to Solars as it was to the people of our world when they first started telling stories about fairies.<br /> <br /> Back then, wasn\u2019t iron just a metal form of mistletoe? Poetically chosen to be the bane of the immortal because of how ridiculously silly it would be to try and make a weapon of it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T10:13:08-07:00","id":1226494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226473#post1226473\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, I wish I was being that clever.<br /> <br /> But no, I just think that people are making this way more complicated than it needs to be. And I\u2019m not referring to the people whose suspension of disbelief is broken because they understand the realities of metallurgy.<br /> <br /> Why should we know why a particular substance is anathema to the Raksha? Why can\u2019t it be a mystery solved different ways at different tables?<br /> <br /> You can't provide an answer to the mystery that will satisfy everyone, and you shouldn\u2019t need to.<br /> <br /> But what you can do is substitute iron for a similarly useless metal that would be just as meaningful within the world of Exalted. A metal as useless to Solars as it was to the people of our world when they first started telling stories about fairies.<br /> <br /> Back then, wasn\u2019t iron just a metal form of mistletoe? Poetically chosen to be the bane of the immortal because of how ridiculously silly it would be to try and make a weapon of it?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Sunder iron was one of the major weapon metals for ages because it could be easily remade and repaired as well as a decently strong material.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T10:18:35-07:00","id":1226496,"title":"","content":"Who/what do you want to be responsible for Raksha-Bane? I think some in universe mythos from differing perspectives would be fun.<br /> <br /> Solars: A solar made it happen of course. To confirm solars in charge and the Creation Ruling Mandate, many diplomatic trips were made into the Wyld to establish peace with early Rakha forces. The first was to establish the rules of negotiation. They displayed the finest jewelry, finery and foods as presents and neutral items to use at the talks. Gold, copper, bronze, the finest steels, glass and stones were displayed to impress the Raksha leaders.<br /> <br /> But, one set of utensils, an old iron soup ladle, offended the fair folk diplomat so badly, he declared that they must not even touch something so hideous, and that to be forced to use it would be considered a violent act. The Eclipse diplomat general saw the use of the Raksha's words and shook on it, locking in one of the earliest agreements with Fair Folk: that ugly Iron is their weakness even to the touch of it.<br /> <br /> More in game mythos later, hard to do this via phone","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T10:44:05-07:00","id":1226504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226494#post1226494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Sunder iron was one of the major weapon metals for ages because it could be easily remade and repaired as well as a decently strong material.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then who was arguing it was uselesly brittle compared to bronze?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T12:21:08-07:00","id":1226544,"title":"","content":"Bronze was better, but that doesn't mean iron was totally useless for making tools and weapons. And the societies that this story comes from (north-west Europe) were not societies that used much bronze.<br /> <br /> Of course, it's probably also noteable that the myths mostly involve not weapons, but things like iron horseshoes over the door (which I still see occasionally on old houses), iron knives under the door, etc, and are actually mostly way, way, after the iron age.<br /> <br /> But that doesn't work so well with fantasy action games.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Anyway, of the ideas suggested so far, I like the Earth essence one the best. Though i can see the value in maybe not getting too technical with it.<br /> Certainly, I can think that in the game, people will have different origin stories they tell of why cold iron harms Raksha... and it could even be that this is what makes it work, the collective legend. Though that's not an answer I'd like, personally.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T13:47:39-07:00","id":1226563,"title":"","content":"The Dragon-Blooded mythos:<br /> <br /> Iron and its powerful connection to Earth Essence was not the only Bane to Fair Folk, its just the only one left. Each element used to have a Bane used to protect its borders. But the Anathema and their snake oil salesmen of diplomats were too lenient on trade agreements, letting the elemental courts who guarded these secrets, and one by one the four directional elements lost their secret weapons. The heated Magnetite of the South, the acid-etched coral blades of the West, no longer proof against the Raksha. Only Iron and itz Courts upon the Blessed Isle stay protected from the Fair Folk and their sneaky types. And only the Immaculate Order can keep the Courts in line and make sure no more concessions are made to the Wyld.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T14:28:06-07:00","id":1226571,"title":"","content":"As a ST for a group, iron works as a bane to fey, because it does. Steel doesn't work as a bane because it doesn't. Attempting to apply metallurgic composition is as ridiculous as people who still believe the Earth is flat. Creation is a world of magic, demons, and gods (among other things). Our modern science doesn't apply. I could ask dozens of questions that science can't answer. Why does leaving gold out in the sun, from dawn to dusk for a 100+ years, turn it into orichalcum? Why can't a shaped fey that entered Creation leave and become unshaped again? How can a person take someone else's shape and memories by eating their heart? I could keep going, but I think I've made my point clear. Science doesn't apply to Creation the way it does to the real world.<br /> <br /> If you're a ST, and you don't like the &quot;iron-bane&quot; schtick, use the golden rule and change it. If you're a player and it bugs you, talk with your ST, but realize he has the final say. Just realize the canon answer is &quot;iron works, steel does not&quot;. It was published this way, and has been reaffirmed by the Devs in the &quot;Ask the Devs&quot; thread. So the canon answer isn't likely to change.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2103,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2103-talosx","user":"TalosX","date":". 06-11-2018, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2103-talosx","name":"TalosX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"588"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T15:29:03-07:00","id":1226582,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TalosX</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226571#post1226571\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a ST for a group, iron works as a bane to fey, because it does. Steel doesn't work as a bane because it doesn't. Attempting to apply metallurgic composition is as ridiculous as people who still believe the Earth is flat. Creation is a world of magic, demons, and gods (among other things). Our modern science doesn't apply. I could ask dozens of questions that science can't answer. Why does leaving gold out in the sun, from dawn to dusk for a 100+ years, turn it into orichalcum? Why can't a shaped fey that entered Creation leave and become unshaped again? How can a person take someone else's shape and memories by eating their heart? I could keep going, but I think I've made my point clear. Science doesn't apply to Creation the way it does to the real world.<br /> <br /> If you're a ST, and you don't like the &amp;quot;iron-bane&amp;quot; schtick, use the golden rule and change it. If you're a player and it bugs you, talk with your ST, but realize he has the final say. Just realize the canon answer is &amp;quot;iron works, steel does not&amp;quot;. It was published this way, and has been reaffirmed by the Devs in the &amp;quot;Ask the Devs&amp;quot; thread. So the canon answer isn't likely to change.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is a big difference between science not being applicable and logic or reason not existing. Yes the difference can be because of magic. But there should be reason and patterns behind it. Someone who is a master of occult or an appropriate lore should know why iron works and not steel or at the very least have a solid theory. DrLoveMonkey explained this far better than I could.<br /> <br /> The issue here is how does the process of making steel differ enough from wrought iron that one is fae bane and the other is not? Its not about house ruling out the percieved contradiction, but about describing an in setting reason why there is no contradiction.<br /> <br /> For some reason I am now imagining situations where fae teach people to make steel just to deal with less iron. Possibly even iron in the ground stabilises against the wyld so they encourage mining of it to get it out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5167-klaek","name":"Klaek","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T15:49:36-07:00","id":1226591,"title":"","content":"The prospect of correcting every person that comes in with misreadings to the effect of &quot;why are you looking for scientific explanations&quot; feels both exhausting and unbelievably tedious, so perhaps we could just have a general moratorium on them?<br /> <br /> (I know we're still going to get them because I'm hardly one to make some kind of official statement, but the last few days have left me quite irritable)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T18:50:03-07:00","id":1226635,"title":"","content":"A summary of the thread (as I understand it):<br /> There is nothing that can be said of steel that cannot also be said of either wrought or cast iron, vis-a-vis how it's worked, the composition, etc. armyofwhispers finds this an interesting topic.<br /> Arguments addressed so far include<ul><li>they're different because they're different, in this fantasy universe<ul><li>Acceptable on a table-by-table basis, but some people are interested in a deeper response.</li> </ul></li> <li>the difference is in their literal name<ul><li>Old Realm has occult significance, and the words are different in Old Realm</li> <li>A sorcerer did a working to make iron dangerous to the fae, and someone else twisted it so steel doesn't count</li> <li>A diplomatic pact was struck that made iron dangerous, but the fae weaseled out of it by pointing out that they didn't specify steel</li> <li>Some combination of the above three.</li> </ul></li> <li>the god of iron hates fae, while the god of steel doesn't<ul><li>That's not technically how gods work, but point taken</li> </ul></li> <li>cold iron is effective because it hasn't been worked<ul><li>Then why do wrought and cast iron work?</li> </ul></li> <li>iron is symbolic of Earth Essence and stasis<ul><li>Then why doesn't white jade work?</li> </ul></li> <li>iron is symbolic of human working and technology<ul><li>Then why doesn't bronze or copper work?</li> </ul></li> <li>steel should work, the devs just don't know metallurgy<ul><li>Petition to add remedial metallurgy courses for the devs as a stretch goal to the next Kickstarter.</li> <li>Also, something about the interesting setup where people use substandard iron weapons in case they have to be used against fae, etc etc.</li> </ul></li> <li>fae are just like that, it's nonsense because they're fae<ul><li>I personally dislike this argument but I guess it is definitionally hard to argue against</li> </ul></li> </ul>It is not my understanding that this thread is an attack on people who don't care about the difference between iron and steel, or even an argument that steel should work. It's wondering if there's an in-universe explanation.<br /> <br /> Personally, I favor the diplomatic explanation, or maybe the sorcerous one. Making iron dangerous to Wyld natives seems like a suitable Solar 3 working.<br /> <br /> EDIT: A couple more explanations I missed: Isator's suggestion that it has to do with a history of charms against fair folk, and Cryo-Seraph's suggestion that there were a group of banes, each symbolically tied to the elements, that were effective, but that only iron remains. For some reason this seems... more compelling to me than the idea that only iron is symbolically effective?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>But sexually.","edited":{"userid":1797,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1797-ellis","user":"Ellis","date":". 06-11-2018, 09:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1797-ellis","name":"Ellis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1797_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"535"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T20:50:36-07:00","id":1226662,"title":"","content":"What if the weakness doesn't come from Creation? Like in Warhammer 40K they once handwaved the fear of death and the image of the Grim Reaper as a side effect of an entity called the Nightbringer that once tormented pretty much all of life to the extent that fear of it was carved into everything's DNA. <br /> <br /> So what if there was some iron monster, something like an Ishvara class Hanya that just sowed such horrors and devastation amongst the fey of it's time that the old horror still reverberates through the fey of the current time. Just a somewhat amusing story idea that came to mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T20:58:41-07:00","id":1226666,"title":"","content":"Random thought: if you <i>do</i> want a partly-sciencey if also kind of ironic reason then steel having the highest percent iron (after accounting for slag inclusions in wrought iron) is probably the way to go. As in, say that the iron bane stops working if the iron is <i>too</i> pure. How could that possibly be? Um... I\u2019m sure someone here can up with something. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","user":"Sith_Happens","date":". 06-12-2018, 12:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T21:06:21-07:00","id":1226671,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sith_Happens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226666#post1226666\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Random thought: if you <i>do</i> want a partly-sciencey I\u2019d rather if also kind of ironic reason then steel having the highest percent iron (after accounting for slag inclusions in wrought iron) is probably the way to go. As in, say that the iron bane stops working if the iron is <i>too</i> pure. How could that possibly be? Um... I\u2019m sure someone here can up with something. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well obviously, its the slag/carbon that's actually the problem. :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T01:44:23-07:00","id":1226712,"title":"","content":"A steel weapon back in the day wasn't just applying the right amount of carbon to the iron, but a lot of pounding to get slag out and even out impurities.<br /> <br /> All of Creation is toxic to the Raksha.<br /> <br /> Iron is a very common element.<br /> <br /> I'm also buying into the idea mentioned by others in this thread, that iron being connected to the element of Earth, and that's why it being dangerous to the Fair Folks. Not because of any story agreed by all in the Wyld, but because it is what makes Creation &quot;Not Wyld&quot;.<br /> <br /> So why iron but not steel? What I will go with myself is that it is not the iron itself, but because that stabilization essence binds to iron. The work of making steel into a useful blade or armor will also drive out said essence. There might be technologies to work steel into useful weapons without driving out the necessary essence, but not yet discovered (or if anyone have, they haven't shared).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T05:28:16-07:00","id":1226738,"title":"","content":"Sidereal Mythos:<br /> <br /> So word around the watercooler, i talked to a gal, knows a guy who knows a draconic censor that has this theory. We used to have all weapons work on the Fair Folk, as all of the metal for the weapons fell under the Bureau of Nature under Luna's purview. Her purview includes Raksha bane.<br /> <br /> But ever since the rise of the War Gods and their move into the Bureau of Heaven, some of those Gods of metallurgy have moved offices, and as they are no longer with the Bureau of Nature, no longer have the access to Luna's right to have her weapons hurt Raksha as well. Copper, Bronze, Steel, and any of the Magical Materials, all fall under uses of War now. Only Wrought Iron and base iron remains, still used primarily for farming implements and looked down upon by the Steel Gods for its brittleness and need for constant repairs.<br /> <br /> The real problem is that most of these Gods don't leave their spots in heaven, leaving gods of the Forge to hang out in Creation for them, so most don't even know that they've lost their rights to be the bane to Raksha.<br /> <br /> But if BlackIce Shadow expects me to send a memo on it without an apology for what he did at my soiree last month, then he can take an extended mission for the Underworld for all i care","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T05:28:27-07:00","id":1226739,"title":"","content":"Leaving aside white jade, if iron was so rich with Earth Essence, it would feature into more Earth-aspected Charms. But those tend to exclude metal more often than not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T05:33:39-07:00","id":1226742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226739#post1226739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Leaving aside white jade, if iron was so rich with Earth Essence, it would feature into more Earth-aspected Charms. But those tend to exclude metal more often than not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well... Earth Essence is more than &quot;the stuff that makes things stable&quot;<br /> <br /> Iron is also, after all, also connected to magnets; another binding force.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T07:45:27-07:00","id":1226778,"title":"","content":"White jade doesn't work because it's too magic. Raksha are stories. They're not hurt any more than normal by legendary weapons, they're hurt by random chunks of metal that nobody would look twice at. <br /> <br /> The random iron poker suddenly becomes a fae slaying iron poker. And it gets that narrative by ripping it out of the fae. That's what does the damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T07:45:59-07:00","id":1226779,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226591#post1226591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The prospect of correcting every person that comes in with misreadings to the effect of &quot;why are you looking for scientific explanations&quot; feels both exhausting and unbelievably tedious, so perhaps we could just have a general moratorium on them?<br /> <br /> (I know we're still going to get them because I'm hardly one to make some kind of official statement, but the last few days have left me quite irritable)</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty much why I haven't posted on this thread in several days. It has become increasingly frustrating trying to explain to people. Plus the occasional person attack are quite wearing. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ellis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226635#post1226635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A summary of the thread (as I understand it):<br /> There is nothing that can be said of steel that cannot also be said of either wrought or cast iron, vis-a-vis how it's worked, the composition, etc. armyofwhispers finds this an interesting topic.<br /> Arguments addressed so far include<ul><li>they're different because they're different, in this fantasy universe<ul><li>Acceptable on a table-by-table basis, but some people are interested in a deeper response.</li> </ul></li> <li>the difference is in their literal name<ul><li>Old Realm has occult significance, and the words are different in Old Realm</li> <li>A sorcerer did a working to make iron dangerous to the fae, and someone else twisted it so steel doesn't count</li> <li>A diplomatic pact was struck that made iron dangerous, but the fae weaseled out of it by pointing out that they didn't specify steel</li> <li>Some combination of the above three.</li> </ul></li> <li>the god of iron hates fae, while the god of steel doesn't<ul><li>That's not technically how gods work, but point taken</li> </ul></li> <li>cold iron is effective because it hasn't been worked<ul><li>Then why do wrought and cast iron work?</li> </ul></li> <li>iron is symbolic of Earth Essence and stasis<ul><li>Then why doesn't white jade work?</li> </ul></li> <li>iron is symbolic of human working and technology<ul><li>Then why doesn't bronze or copper work?</li> </ul></li> <li>steel should work, the devs just don't know metallurgy<ul><li>Petition to add remedial metallurgy courses for the devs as a stretch goal to the next Kickstarter.</li> <li>Also, something about the interesting setup where people use substandard iron weapons in case they have to be used against fae, etc etc.</li> </ul></li> <li>fae are just like that, it's nonsense because they're fae<ul><li>I personally dislike this argument but I guess it is definitionally hard to argue against</li> </ul></li> </ul>It is not my understanding that this thread is an attack on people who don't care about the difference between iron and steel, or even an argument that steel should work. It's wondering if there's an in-universe explanation.<br /> <br /> Personally, I favor the diplomatic explanation, or maybe the sorcerous one. Making iron dangerous to Wyld natives seems like a suitable Solar 3 working.<br /> <br /> EDIT: A couple more explanations I missed: Isator's suggestion that it has to do with a history of charms against fair folk, and Cryo-Seraph's suggestion that there were a group of banes, each symbolically tied to the elements, that were effective, but that only iron remains. For some reason this seems... more compelling to me than the idea that only iron is symbolically effective?</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you very much for summing this up! <br /> <br /> I think my personal favorite has been the pair solar workings adding in iron and then subtracting steel (or changing the definition of steel such that it doesn't count), but a fair few have been compelling for one reason or another. The people who are actually willing to discuss the topic in good faith have provided some interesting ideas.<br /> <br /> I don't think I'll be commenting on this topic henceforth though. We had some good discussion here but, the constant barrage of people just posting to thread crap is past my point of tolerance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T10:34:34-07:00","id":1226836,"title":"","content":"Another science-based seed for inspiration - Iron is the start of the breaking point where neither fusion nor fission produces more energy than what's required to overcome their internal binding forces. In real life, it and nickel are also a driving force for the heat death of the universe. Obviously, we can't use much of this because nickel isn't also harming the Fair Folk and Creation only resembles IRL down to the cellular level and maybe slightly smaller than that, so any discussion of subatomic processes is a no go, but something might come out of it, who knows.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226739#post1226739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Leaving aside white jade, if iron was so rich with Earth Essence, it would feature into more Earth-aspected Charms. But those tend to exclude metal more often than not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, if that's the case, why not salt, which is specifically mentioned in a number of Earth keyword Charms? It clearly isn't just Earth Essence, because it has a toggle switch on &quot;kill fae&quot; and &quot;kill dead&quot;, and if you're pounding the slag out, that strikes me as very Fire.<br /> <br /> It could also be a Ravana deal where the Fair Folk see the use of weapons of the gods (long before humans were a thing) incorporating steel or bronze and make themselves resistant to those, but didn't think to proof themselves against pots, pans, horseshoes, or other crude constructions that humans eventually hammered out much later. Oh, the ignominy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T10:36:05-07:00","id":1226837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226778#post1226778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">White jade doesn't work because it's too magic. Raksha are stories. They're not hurt any more than normal by legendary weapons, they're hurt by random chunks of metal that nobody would look twice at.<br /> <br /> The random iron poker suddenly becomes a fae slaying iron poker. And it gets that narrative by ripping it out of the fae. That's what does the damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Earth Essence is magic, though. The basic building blocks of the setting are magical. If crappy iron harms Fair Folk, therefore, it is also magical.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T10:59:06-07:00","id":1226847,"title":"","content":"Yes, but the Magical Materials are, as a rule, more magical than other sorts of materials. <br /> <br /> Hence the name.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T11:09:42-07:00","id":1226855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226847#post1226847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but the Magical Materials are, as a rule, more magical than other sorts of materials.<br /> <br /> Hence the name.</div> </div> </div> </div>It still doesn't explain how it would impede the beef that Earth has with the Wyld, unless there was something more than just needing Earth Essence which, again, is likely, since salt affects the dead but not Fair Folk..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T12:11:15-07:00","id":1226873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference#post1225276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wrought iron: Extremely low carbon content by weight ~ 0.08% carbon. Also is alloyed with slag (up to 2% by weight). Slag is a byproduct of the transition from iron ore to iron. Better than pure iron, but only because the slag gives it some kind of structure. The carbon here provides some structure but not a whole lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nitpick/trivia: wrought iron isn't alloyed with the slag. The slag (mostly ferrous silicates) takes the form of superfine glassy fibers embedded in the near - pure iron matrix, producing a composite material: basically, metal fiberglass. This was actually known to the ancients, albeit not in explicit detail, because if you try to work wrought iron too cold it splinters like wood and it's called 'wrought' iron because you have to pound the hell out of the smelting product over and over again to get the slag to 'disappear' so you had something workable for tool and detail use. The embedded fibers also give it superior anti-corrosion properties on par with stainless steels.<br /> <br /> And to the point of the post, yes, it seems very silly to equate cast iron and pure iron but not steel, even narratively, because cast iron was always known to be the least pure form.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1096-squidheadjax","name":"squidheadjax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1096_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T15:32:53-07:00","id":1226960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226779#post1226779\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I think my personal favorite has been the pair solar workings adding in iron and then subtracting steel (or changing the definition of steel such that it doesn't count)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure that it's not a thing that should be possible, though; it's far too extreme to count as a subtle alteration to the cosmos.<br /> <br /> \u200bThink about the precedent it sets; if Solar workings can designate an entire class of being, that are actually magical no less, is vulnerable to a mundane substance that can cause aggravated damage and disrupt their powers, can one also be used to render people (up to and including other Exalted?) who are in political or ideological dispute with you to take acidic damage from contact with the air?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T18:56:32-07:00","id":1227008,"title":"","content":"Lunar mythos:<br /> <br /> I will tell you the secret of Iron, but you must keep it to yourself. The entire thing comes from a nearly forgotten tale of great heroism and tragedy which i will not repeat for obvious reasons later. A powerful Raksha and his 'brother' were out conquering all they could before the times of the Exalted. Luna knew together the two were unbeatable, so she became the middle of a triangle to split them up.<br /> <br /> She cozied up to both often, but while she got the weaker one to let slip one secret, the other would not budge. So she used his one weakness, his dislike for the fuzzy feeling he got from magnetized iron, and crushed him with a small mountain of it, then calcified and ruined his remaining essence.<br /> <br /> When she returned to the weaker brother, he confronted her about his brother. She said she slew him by driving a single iron nail into him (not mentioning the size of said nail). Then she smiled her wickedest grin and added,&quot; and guess what he told me about your weaknesses?&quot; and that Raksha ran and never looked back, but the tale got told over and again until Iron worked on all Fae. But if the truth got out that it took a mountain of it, the tale would unravel and the Bane would be lost.<br /> <br /> So keep your mouth shut about it, and tell them the b.s. the DBs think of instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24220-cryo-seraph","name":"Cryo-Seraph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T21:53:26-07:00","id":1227046,"title":"","content":"Simple historical option: the fae bane of iron came into being before steelmaking was widespread in Creation, and only applies to the kinds of \u201ciron\u201d that existed at that time. Had steel been adopted earlier then perhaps things would be different, but alas, it\u2019s far too late for that now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T05:32:13-07:00","id":1227116,"title":"","content":"I see a rather big question among the posts; is iron inherently dangerous to fair folks, or is it story driven?<br /> <br /> Only the inherent option makes the follow up question of &quot;why not steel?&quot; a problematic one. <br /> <br /> However, in my opinion, the story driven one gives follow up questions of &quot;why not other materials, like twig of a willow&quot; and how come all of them are. Or are some of them immune, because they never got the memo?<br /> <br /> As someone running with the &quot;creatures from the Wyld are inherently vulnerable to iron&quot; theme, none of the &quot;iron works but steel doesn't is because the legend and stories between them&quot; will not cut it.<br /> <br /> In the same way, I assume the &quot;well, material composition and crystalline structures, combined with essence of X&quot; is a very boring bla, bla, bla answer to anyone preferring a &quot;because the story of Y&quot; reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T07:47:07-07:00","id":1227141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1226960#post1226960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm pretty sure that it's not a thing that should be possible, though; it's far too extreme to count as a subtle alteration to the cosmos.<br /> <br /> \u200bThink about the precedent it sets; if Solar workings can designate an entire class of being, that are actually magical no less, is vulnerable to a mundane substance that can cause aggravated damage and disrupt their powers, can one also be used to render people (up to and including other Exalted?) who are in political or ideological dispute with you to take acidic damage from contact with the air?</div> </div> </div> </div>I realize that the Solar Ambition 3 description states 'subtle' alterations, but the description of solar circle workings in general states &quot;they can rewrite the laws of reality, or write new ones into being.&quot; The scope this suggests is not subtle and I honestly think it conflicts with the description of ambition 3.<br /> <br /> That said, it's possible that the working can still be subtle. We don't actually know what the base state was before the change. Perhaps the Raksha always had some weakness or flaw but it was individual to each Raksha. This makes sense to me as Achilles heel or Balder's weakness to mistletoe are well known stories of flaw or weakness. An invincible enemy just doesn't make a good story. If this was the case, perhaps the working was simply to alter that state such that instead of each Raksha having a separate one, they all suffer from iron weakness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T11:15:43-07:00","id":1227178,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar8804_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"8804\" data-vbnamecard=\"8804\">armyofwhispers</a> Vance in the Q&amp;A thread the 1E interpretation of the Salanian working is the basis for defining ambition 3 solar. Now obviously you can golden rule but since he is the one who wrote the Working system, I think this is probably the standard for gauging what constitutes subtle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T17:15:02-07:00","id":1227275,"title":"","content":"It might be helpful if we knew where iron actually came from.<br /> <br /> Quicksilver might be an instructive comparison. The LED known as the Quicksilver Queen, ruler of the Mercury Ants, presides over mercury in Creation. At one point in the past mercury had various beneficial properties such as being used in life extension thaumaturgy. Then a Solar betrayed her, she got mad, and now mercury is poison.<br /> <br /> So fairly fundamental properties of pure substances can be changed. Now I don't know if the Queen and/or the Ants makes all the quicksilver in Creation, but if they do an analogous process for iron might explain its anti-fey properties. Whoever is responsible for it could endow it with that virtue somehow.<br /> <br /> As for why steel, doesn't work, perhaps it is magical? By which I mean, basically all material objects in Creation are produced by hand. If Creation's chemistry is alchemy, then the intent involved in the thaumaturgical/alchemical procedure and/or the spiritual refinement involved in the smith might be what changes the material.<br /> <br /> In fact... what if steel is _supposed_ to retain the anti-fey virtue of iron but for whatever reason it is being made wrong in the Second Age? The god of steel or some other important entity may be dead, imprisoned, etc. or have levied a curse. I mean this to be distinct from a working and more related to the innate influence some entities like gods have over their domain. The replacement god of steel who replaced the solar loyalist may not be capable of the job and preserving the strength of steel over its iron-like damage to fey was one of the tradeoffs that needed to be made.<br /> <br /> Or it could be as simple as the proper procedure was lost or was too expensive and the current method is what was rediscovered or cobbled together. If the ritual required a sprinkling of gold dust and a long prayer to preserve a property most people never use it may have been skipped.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T18:38:30-07:00","id":1227304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1227141#post1227141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize that the Solar Ambition 3 description states 'subtle' alterations, but the description of solar circle workings in general states &amp;quot;they can rewrite the laws of reality, or write new ones into being.&amp;quot; The scope this suggests is not subtle and I honestly think it conflicts with the description of ambition 3.</div> </div> </div> </div>On its own, the introduction doesn't offer any scope at all; we only actually get that when it gets to describing projects, and things that are capable of affecting everything are limited to the subtle.<br /> <br /> That's kind of necessary to prevent reality from becoming a free-for-all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T19:07:51-07:00","id":1227314,"title":"","content":"What if a culture doesn't meaningfully differentiate between iron and steel, and steel made by them does act as a Raksha bane?<br /> <br /> But looking at this from a Out of Character perspective, what do we want to accomplish with iron being the bane of the Fair Folk, but not steel? It seems to me that we definitely don't want every artifact weapon to act as a fae-bane, simply by virtue of alloying the magical material with steel in its creation. We don't even want every good steel weapon to act as a bane. So perhaps its the very quality of making it weapon-grade iron that makes it no longer a bane: iron must be too soft, or too brittle, to be generally useful to fight the fae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1581-tonberrian","name":"tonberrian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-13T19:31:33-07:00","id":1227320,"title":"","content":"Hm. Option 1: change cold iron to lead. Takes care of the steel problem, and lead has some legends of its own. Not the most elegant of solutions, but works.<br /> <br /> Option 2: In Creation, &quot;Steel&quot; means iron alloyed with a very small amount of jade. Jade weapons are iron with a much hirer percent jade, such that only exalted can shape the material. Why is pure iron anti-fae? It was the preferred element (a la theian to) of a now-dead Primordial who kicked much fae ass. The Prim is dead, but its element lives on. Or something about how Alchemy in Creation has iron as the &quot;dead end&quot; element - you can turn other elements into iron, but can't turn iron into anything else (at least not without a &quot;philosophers stone&quot; like jade), making it the ultimate anti-change metal<br /> <br /> Option 3: IIRC, in Roman times you had two ways to work with iron. Either build a kind of mud kiln and heat the ore until it melted and ran out the bottom, or stick it in a pile of charcoal and pump a lot of air in. The end result being either a lot of carbon in the mix, or burning all the carbon out. To get steel you either started with special iron that had the right elements already mixed in, and kept the iron cool enough to not lose the alloy (wootz) or you used your secret recipe to get just the right amount of carbon in. So in Creation steel would have human intention and a thaumaturgy ritual, while iron is just &quot;heat the stuff and hammer away&quot;. Why that would make it worse against the fae I dunno.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2678-spoonr","name":"SpoonR","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2678_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"152"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-14T13:30:48-07:00","id":1227508,"title":"","content":"Having watched this discussion spiral around the same themes for the last nine pages, I decided, as my first post on these forums, to post out my view on the matter. Especially because I find this thread having potential, should it be directed more constructively.<br /> <br /> First I shall talk about the most likely reason why everyone keeps on runnign to iron as an abhorrence for the fae in the <i>Exalted</i>. I would not like to do this kind of wall of text, but as the originator of this post wants a DEFINITIVE answer on a feature in a fantasy RPG setting, and has refused to consider whether this feat is impossible, I feel I need to try and explain this from the beginning, as these themes have been touched in this post before, but they have either not been understood or they have not been explained well enough.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Contrary to some strange notions on this thread, the idea of harmful Iron has been around longer than the <b>Kipling </b>poem that was mentioned. <b>Kipling </b>wrote this poem on 1910, but beliefs and superstitions regarding iron have been around much longer. I am unsure why the originator of this post pointed this poem akin to a source on the &quot;cold iron&quot; myth on modern days. I am unsure if he ment it as so, but it came across like it. However, it is a good example about the problematic position on the nature of iron.<br /> <br /> The abhorrent nature of iron has been around far longer. <i>The Bible's Book of Judges</i> has a fairly famous verse (Judges 1:19) where God is unable to bring the men of Judah victory over an enemy for the stated reason that they had &quot;chariots of iron&quot;. Many historians consider it an anachronism by the author writing centuries later, since at the time of the battle iron-wrought chariots would have been unheard of. As it turns out, other mythological sources have similiar takes on the nature of iron as something that repels the magical and the unexplainable. The Islamic djinn were also said to dread iron. Also in the norse <i>Saga of Hervor and Heidrek</i> from the 13th century, King Svafrlami catches two dwarfs by using iron. Thus, the idea of iron as harmful to supernatural has been around for good thousand plus years, and in different cultures.<br /> <br /> I would argue that even the idea that iron was harmful only to faeries is not technically so. Or in actuality, our perception on what counts as a &quot;faerie&quot; has changed. Djinn, for example, are not &quot;faerie&quot; in the sense that we understand. I argue, that the term was used in medieval times to have more widespread meaning than what we give it today. For example, in the book Faerie Folklore in Medieval Tales \u2013 an Introduction, it is mentioned that during middle ages such creatures as banshees were counted among the numbers of the fae or fair folk. Creatures which modern popular culture labels as undead. Also during much modern times, in 1910 novel <i>Phantom of the Opera</i>, a book from <b>Gaston Leroux</b> that later inspired the musical of the same name by Andrew Lloyd Webber, everyone at the Paris Opera relies on iron for protection against &quot;the ghost.&quot; I point out these examples as a reminder, that even though Exalted &quot;fair folk&quot; are not faeries as we understand such notions, neither are all the creatures of the mythology that people of those earlier times considered as such. Thus the term &quot;fair folk&quot; is more akin to &quot;otherworldly&quot; and &quot;unnatural&quot; than faeries with wings. This, I think, Exalted takes very well into account.<br /> <br /> Many modern fantasy writers have used iron as harmful to faeries since <b>Kiplings </b>poem and <i>Phantom of the Opera</i>. Most notably such masters as <b>Terry Pratchett</b> in his <i>Discworld</i> series, <b>Neil</b> <b>Gaiman</b> in his <i>Stardust </i>book, <b>Robert Jordan </b>in his <i>Wheel of Time</i> series and <b>David Eddings </b>in his <i>Elenium </i>and the <i>Tamuli </i>series. Many more slightly less- known fantasy authors fall into using cold iron rather than steel as a weapons against the supernatural. <b>Patrick Rothfuss' </b><i>The Name of the Wind</i>, <b>Josepha Sherman's </b><i>A Strange and Ancient Name</i>. <b>T.H White's </b><i>Once and Future King </i>and many many more. Many RPG:s such as <i>Dungeons and Dragons</i>, the one that lift the RPG genre into prominence, have similiar notions on cold iron - material harming the fey creatures. And <i>Exalteds </i>previous company, White Wolf's RPG: <i>Changeling </i>worked closely on this faerie theme and the abhorence on iron. WoD IS relevant, read on.<br /> <br /> Examples above testify, that the idea of iron as a harmful force against the supernatural, rather than steel, has been engraved into our conception of mythology. Steel is used in the modern times, and thus automatically is more &quot;real&quot; and as such less suitable in creating such mythologically epic narrative as iron. Whether the iron in the ancient times or the stories really was iron or steel, IS IRRELEVANT (this is very important). The important is that it was PERCEIVED as iron by MODERN writers and the majoritys interpretation on mythology and folklore.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Why is the above relevant? The narrative, the story, rather than scientific metallurgy explains the usage of cold iron in a fantasy work better. <i>Exalted </i>was not invented let alone written in a vacuum. It is, as is every book and text, influenced by others that came before it and the cultural norms and understanding of its writers and desingers. Being a combination of all kind of features from different mythologies, I find it not strange at all that our take on mythological &quot;iron hurts faeries&quot; trope has found its way to <i>Exalted</i>. In my opinion, it is best to not wonder why, but instead how to incorporate the questions that rise from contradictions into ones own games'. Into the mythological and epic stories that are <i>Exalted</i>. <br /> <br /> In short: the real answer to the question &quot;why&quot; is: Because it has been so in our collective mythology and popular culture for a long time and has formed the majority of our mythological understanding to be such. It is because it is.<br /> <br /> To get this post REALLY on the subject that I think is most important here. While demanding a DEFINITIVE answer might be a bit silly to ask from someone other than the developers, and can also be very restrictive to peoples playstyles, should one demand the existence of one (for it implies that this is the one true way). I find it better to find a BUNCH of answers and then everyone to decide for themselves. Again, the narrative, the story, needs to take precedence over metallurgy and physics.<br /> <br /> Having said that, the question IS an interesting one. WHY iron in the first place? Why would the fair folk, the raksha, be especially hurt by any material AT ALL? We might find something to guide us by from the 2nd edition book <i>Graceful Wicked Masques.</i>While game-mechanically horrible even at the time of 2nd edition, lore-wise the book is of good quality. As the originator of this post generously commented, 2E is perfectly valid source, and some fair folk aspects from 2E have already been suggested to be transferred to this edition in <i>Arms of the Chosen</i>. We must wait and see, but for now, this is with what we must go by:<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The originator of this post has the notion that the raksha are some sort of beings akin to the <i>Exalted </i>gods or a form of demon. Beings who happen to hate creation, but from different reasons as the other enemies of the gods. Let me be absolutely clear so that the nature of raksha (according to the earlier editions of the <i>Exalted </i>ofcourse) does not come into question again.<br /> <br /> The MAIN thing about the fair folk is that they indeed do not understand. (As someone pointed out).<br /> <br /> They do not understand any of it. Raksha do not understand anything that lies within Creation, nor any concept inside Creation. They do not understand the concept of sustenance (even though they require it while in creation), they do not understand the concept of &quot;life&quot; (even though they are by some standards, alive) and even their understanding of &quot;existence&quot; is so alien as to be unfathomable to mortals. They do not understand any of the concepts of Creation.<br /> <br /> Perhaps the raksha understand permanence least of all. Raksha are definately not permanent as there is no such thing as &quot;permanence&quot; in the wyld. Passing of time as a concept is the only thing that raksha can even remotely relate to due to the first Shinma, but they did not have any deeper understanding on history, and thus of growing old. To Raksha, there was only &quot;now&quot; and a passing of infinite &quot;now&quot;:s. The matter of being permanent was not an issue because they would not cease to exist in the future since there was no future. Nor did they contemplate their beginning since there was no past. There was only now. Thus they are not by any means &quot;ageless&quot;, yet alone consider themselves as such, since there is no age in the Wyld, not fully.<br /> <br /> It all changed with Creation somewhat. In creation there is such concepts which raksha did not previously encounter, and the appearance of Creation changed the Wyld in the process. Wyld still is not permanent and there is no history as we understand it, but the concepts of them appeared. While these concepts were unfathomable to the raksha, they still saw them in action in Creation. Thus the raksha are able to work akin to these concepts even though they understand them not at all.<br /> <br /> For the raksha are excellent at pretending. Everything a raksha does, it pretends. EVERYTHING. The fair folk in <i>Exalted </i>are endlessly malleable beings and should you encounter one, it pretends all its actions, from breathing to eating (if it remembers to have such things), from rage to hapiness and other feelings. Even the fact that it falls towards the ground when dropped from high is pretense. It understands not why these things should happen, yet alone how, but it knows that they are &quot;just how things happen&quot; in creation and thus need to abide by them. <br /> <br /> Most simple to imagine a raksha is that the raksha are beings of infinite potential and possibilities, who in wyld can do anything, and be anything by merely a whim. They are bundles of conciousness in a sea of infinite potential they alone can control. Raksha in Creation are trapped in a game where there are rules. They do not understand the rules or see the point of the rules, but they will calcify if they ignore or do not heed the rules. Thus they play by the rules. They would stop at any time, should they wish to, but then the Creation would impose on them and calcify them (&quot;death&quot; is not a good enough word of what happens to raksha when it &quot;dies&quot; for it is not alive as we see such things, thus the word calcification). This is one reason why they hate Creation so much. It imposes on them rules, and in the Wyld there is no rules. They do not understand how, why, when or where or anything of these rules. They just know the rules are (if they truly grasp the idea what &quot;are&quot;, &quot;is&quot;, &quot;being&quot; or &quot;existing&quot; even mean).<br /> <br /> When a raksha, for whatever reason, comes to creation, it enters through the Gates of Sundraprishna. This is not a place, for the Wyld has no specific place, and I understand that neither is it a specific method of entering Creation, for the raksha have no grasp of a single way to do anything. Gates of Sundraprishna is a methaphysical choice. The raksha chooses what kind of being it will pretend to be. What kind of skills it pretends to have, what kind of goals it might want, what kind of person it is and what it hopes to achieve. Most importantly, it chooses what kind of story it wants. <br /> <br /> THIS defines the raksha IN Creation. It defines everything the raksha is from that point on until it returns back to the Wyld and its being of potentially anything at a whim. A courtesan raksha has chosen to be a courtesan, and cannot suddenly choose to be a spouse, a mother, father or anything else than a courtesan. Creation-born entities might give it these labels, like in our game where one of our players married one. But it will never BE a wife. It has chosen to be a courtesan and have a courtesans story. As a courtesan it will remain until it changes its role, changes it's entire being in Creation.<br /> <br /> The Gates of Sundraprishna allows the raksha to work in the creation without instant calcification by agreeing to follow the rules. Some rules are more universal than others. Such as no raksha can enter Creation indestructible. Is this a defense mechanism of Creation left by the Primordials, or because nothing in Creation is truly indestructible and as such Creation imposes this rule on everything in it? Your guess is as good as mine. However, as you can see, there are many MORE rules than just iron-vulnerability that applies to ALL the fair folk.<br /> <br /> Nothing else in Creation bears such a vulnerability to iron as the fair folk, however. This is extremely interesting. Because Creation lacks any other such distinctive ruling on a metal being especially harmful, it must be something the raksha choose when entering the Gates of Sundraprishna.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Thus we come to the question that this post should have been asking all along: What could it be that has gotten and continues to get ALL the raksha to CHOOSE that iron, cold iron, master of them all, is a substance that specifically hurts them when they pass through the Gates of Sundraprishna? <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24315-shensi","name":"Shensi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-14T14:21:50-07:00","id":1227523,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shensi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1227508#post1227508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Contrary to some strange notions on this thread, the idea of harmful Iron has been around longer than the <b>Kipling </b>poem that was mentioned. <b>Kipling </b>wrote this poem on 1910, but beliefs and superstitions regarding iron have been around much longer. I am unsure why the originator of this post pointed this poem akin to a source on the &quot;cold iron&quot; myth on modern days.</div> </div> </div> </div>You appear to have fundamentally misunderstood my point. I was not arguing that Kipling originated the idea that iron was a bane against the supernatural. That would be nonsensical, since the poem doesn't actually mention <i>any</i> sort of harmful effect iron has on supernatural beings. I was pointing out that the idea of &quot;cold iron&quot; as some sort of special iron, as distinct from regular iron or steel, seems to stem from a misreading of this poem.<br /> <br /> My main point, however, is that the folklore about iron, which definitely does date back to long before the 19th century, <i>doesn't make any distinction between iron and steel</i>. I'll reiterate the example of burying a knife to ward off supernatural threats: knives would have been <i>made of steel, </i>at least any decent knife blade would have. And the superstition doesn't say &quot;bury a knife to ward off evil, but only a <i>cheap</i> knife. If it's good, it won't work&quot;. <br /> <br /> You've provided lots of examples of iron working against the supernatural. That's great, but that's not the question anyone's really been asking. The question was &quot;why do things named iron work against the Fair Folk, but not steel, when several of the materials that do work share more properties in common with steel than &quot;pure&quot; iron, or are more divergent from &quot;pure&quot; iron than steel itself is&quot;. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shensi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Examples above testify, that the idea of iron as a harmful force against the supernatural, rather than steel, has been engraved into our conception of mythology. Steel is used in the modern times, and thus automatically is more &quot;real&quot; and as such less suitable in creating such mythologically epic narrative as iron. Whether the iron in the ancient times or the stories really was iron or steel, IS IRRELEVANT (this is very important). The important is that it was PERCEIVED as iron by MODERN writers and the majoritys interpretation on mythology and folklore.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't believe the idea that steel is somehow perceived as more &quot;modern&quot; is particularly true - myths and legends about steel date back a long, long time (some of the claims made about Wootz steel, for example, are barely centuries younger than the general introduction of iron). Even if I conceded that point, however, I don't accept that, when creating a narrative or setting, have to accept only ideas that are prevalent in modern culture. It's okay to use older narrative structures, if they're interesting or just make more sense with the setting. To use another example, Exalted puts a lot of emphasis on the ideas that the protagonists are &quot;heroes in the Greek style&quot;, focusing on their larger-than-life exploits and divorcing the idea of &quot;heroism&quot; from &quot;morality&quot;. That idea of heroism is definitely not a particularly modern conceit - most modern fiction pretty firmly attaches the idea of a hero to someone who is a good person. We've created a word, &quot;anti-hero&quot;, to describe the opposite, in fact, a protagonist capable of great deeds but who's morally conflicted or just outright bad. Exalted says &quot;No, the Greeks were right, those are just 'heroes'&quot;. <br /> <br /> So, Exalted as a game line and a work of fiction is entirely capable of using non-modern ideas and integrating them into the setting and narrative it's trying to build. So, I see no reason why the modern, artificial distinction between &quot;iron&quot; and &quot;steel&quot; should be preserved. While it's certainly possible to come up with some justifications for it, and some people in the thread have definitely done so, I think it's just easier to dump it entirely. Why keep it, if it isn't historical, and just requires convoluted explanations for the difference? What narrative purpose does making the distinction serve? Are there great tales that can be told when iron and steel affect the Raksha differently, that can't be done if they're the same? <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-15T00:07:08-07:00","id":1227600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1227523#post1227523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> What narrative purpose does making the distinction serve? Are there great tales that can be told when iron and steel affect the Raksha differently, that can't be done if they're the same?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the distinction means the choice between <i>bronze or steel</i> weapons is <i>purely cosmetic</i> and we don't have to do <i>quite as much</i> hand waving to explain why Creation still uses bronze weapons. Where as cold iron at least has the <i>narrative</i> of being inferior weapon material.<br /> <br /> As it stands, we can just say iron is just a little rarer than copper and tin in Creation, perhaps most abundant on the Blessed Isle where the fair folk aren't, but not so rare that cold iron (or steel) is almost considered a magical material.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":487,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","user":"Greyman","date":". 06-15-2018, 03:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-19T18:55:48-07:00","id":1228730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225327#post1225327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other issue is that cast iron, pure iron and wrought iron all <i>really </i>suck as weapons. Cast iron because it would break the second you hit anything other than a fae (assuming it behaves like steel vs fae. If not it would break then too), pure iron because it's hard to cut things when you're as sharp as a butter knife at best, and wrought iron because the very impurities of slag that give it some structure are also exactly where it's gonna break after some use. So I guess you could argue that iron vs fae is effective only when it's not very effective otherwise but then what if I hit him with a steel pot strapped to a tree branch? Does that count?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes it does count. In one of the 1st Ed fictions, a guy hits one of the Fae with an iron hoe, which does more damage than everything else used up to that point.<br /> <br /> As to the whole iron/steel concept, I find that the Fae, who are living stories, have iron as a weakness, and not steel. Why? Because one of them took that as a weakness, and it stuck. When your whole existence is a story, you can make decisions that don't make scientific sense, but do make story sense. &quot;This stuff that makes poor weapons, yeah, I'm vulnerable to that, not the better worked stuff called something else.&quot; Like how Superman is vulnerable to Kryptonite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24266-aoi-cobalt","name":"Aoi Cobalt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-19T20:47:14-07:00","id":1228741,"title":"","content":"I would also point out that iron swords/weapons aren't completely terrible. Sure a sword made entirely of iron is weak and liable to break easily, but you only need the edge of the sword to be iron. In both eastern and western history, the highest quality swords were usually forged from multiple types of steel. They do this by forging the core of the blade, and the edge of the blade separately. There's no reason this can't be applied to Creation. In my Creation, iron weapons necessary to fight the fae, would have steel forged cores, with an iron edge. Sure the weapons aren't as strong as proper steel and bronze weapons, and they'll require more maintenance. They wouldn't break very easily though.<br /> <br /> I would also point out, fae still take lethal damage from most steel weapons. A steel axe is going to hurt them just as much as most other beings. Just because iron does aggravated damage, doesn't mean you have to use it. We had a Night and Zenith duo in my current group, that wiped out multiple BGs of goblins and several Ogres. Neither of them had any iron, the Zenith used his bare fists!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2103,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2103-talosx","user":"TalosX","date":". 06-19-2018, 11:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2103-talosx","name":"TalosX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"588"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-21T07:37:59-07:00","id":1229146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1225763#post1225763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'll take a stab at this (though I feel I'm repeating other people):<br /> <br /> It's not iron that's special, it's steel. Steel inspires people with the possibilities of what metallurgy can accomplish as the useless or limited utility forms of iron are transformed into one of the most potent technologies humanity has ever discovered. But the awe and wonder of what steel can do, means the Raksha can interact with it safely unlike other forms of iron.<br /> <br /> This is consistent with the setting, because of the dream-narrative nature of fae creatures. While steel isn't to the level of the standard magical materials, it could easily be called the first magical material that humans could access without thaumaturgy, sorcerery or those that naturally work Essence. Like the major magical materials, and the other minor special materials out there, their special standing in human imagination is strong enough that fae creatures aren't harmed directly by it.<br /> <br /> It makes sense because the idea of steel being 'special' in some fashion beyond chemical superiority is something we see in real world human mythology. Multiple cultures have myths about the steel produced in meteors, or the Greek concept of &quot;shining iron,&quot; so on. While steel was in the phase of becoming commonly understood as a thing to make, but still before mass production, it grew to have a special place in the minds of people. Even today people have illogical assumptions about how special Damascus steel and Japanese folded steel are (I mean, they look cool, but they're about turning mediocre steel into good steel, not good steel into awesome steel). In Creation there's no reason to assume there wasn't a similar psychological process. Steel is special, steel is what elevates metal working to a civilization growing tool.<br /> <br /> If that's not good enough... I'm not sure what any of us can really say.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly I think the above idea is the most consistent and satisfying answer that's been articulated in the thread so far. This discussion has gone over and over how it doesn't make any sense, chemically speaking, for steel not to count when cast iron and wrought iron do, so obviously it's not the chemical structure of various forms of iron and steel. Attempts to justify iron as a paragon of Earth essence stumble over why iron has this special property and not other contenders, and explanations to do with adding too much Fire essence during the process of creating steel run afoul of cast iron's inclusion in the list of faerie banes. <br /> <br /> The only consistent difference is in the way human culture thinks and speaks about iron and steel. Steel is a technological marvel that represents the current peak of metallurgy, and its production is regarded as an arcane process. There's an element of wonder there. <br /> <br /> So you have iron. It's the bane of the Fair Folk, for whatever reason. I don't think the &quot;why&quot; of the iron bane was ever the main point under discussion, so we'll take it as read. Some innate property of iron makes it baneful to raksha. You melt and cast it, still baneful. Heat and hammer it any way you like, alloy it with whatever, as long as it's still predominantly iron, still baneful. So steel should count, yes? Yes. Except that because of cultural attitudes to steel, for the purposes of the raksha what you're doing when you create steel, with those cutting edge production methods that produce this marvelously useful and valuable material, is alloying iron not just with carbon but with <i>human wonder</i>. And that shit is delicious. That's what deprives steel of its banefulness. <br /> <br /> What I wonder is, in that case, would steel become a bane again when it becomes easier to produce and more widespread, and thus less wondrous? Or does its history as a marvelous metal of arcane craftsmanship mean its bane status is lost forever?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-21T10:06:38-07:00","id":1229172,"title":"","content":"I'd say steel left a mark that industrialization didn't kill. That's why I referenced the modern myths around things like Damascus and Japanese folded steels. Mass production simple shifted the &quot;specialness&quot; of steel to techniques that are more commonly done by individual smiths (generally just mistaking the time and energy of someone working something as an art) as superior to the techniques used to facilitate effective mass production.<br /> <br /> I think the bigger question would be over time does iron vs. steel move on to &quot;artisan&quot; vs. mass production. We see, for example, cast iron getting a huge cultural boost these days as people relearn that it makes great cookware that lasts far longer with far less issues than chemically coated non-stick steel. The shift is more towards &quot;old ways were better&quot; thinking than, &quot;steel isn't special.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-26T16:45:30-07:00","id":1230397,"title":"","content":"Part of what makes the iron vulnerability so weird is that the concept of a particular type of spirit having a unique bane fell by the wayside as the gameline expanded, particularly once making wards became a codified skill.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T06:51:13-07:00","id":1230525,"title":"","content":"You mean like how ghosts are vulnerable to salt, but this doesn't mean a lot now without particular charms/techniques?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T07:08:24-07:00","id":1230530,"title":"","content":"I always assumed the weakness was to wrought iron was a part and parcel of the Creation Spanning Trade Network... no, that's not its name, but the name escapese me.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I assumed that for narrative reasons the Raksha were weak to wrought iron because of its link to bureaucracy, institutions, and the mechanical realities of agrarian society. Horseshoes, fences, piping, ovens; these things subsume individual stories beneath the heel of a larger bureaucracy that works to sweep away individual assumptions for stable canon.<br /> <br /> Is the out of game basis due to a bad reading of a Kipling poem decades prior to D&amp;D as we know it? Prolly, but I think the idea resonates with a contemporary rp gamer in a way that keeps the tradition going even if it relies on bad myth reading.<br /> <br /> It tells you something interesting about who the Raksha are, which I think is one of the most important things mechanical representations should do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron-crowns.obsidianportal.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron...dianportal.com</a> Visit Golden Shackles, Iron Crowns; a 3rd Edition Exalted Campaign ongoing since 2014","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2711-janissary87","name":"Janissary87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2711_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T07:19:24-07:00","id":1230533,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Measure of Hope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1229146#post1229146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Honestly I think the above idea is the most consistent and satisfying answer that's been articulated in the thread so far. This discussion has gone over and over how it doesn't make any sense, chemically speaking, for steel not to count when cast iron and wrought iron do, so obviously it's not the chemical structure of various forms of iron and steel. Attempts to justify iron as a paragon of Earth essence stumble over why iron has this special property and not other contenders, and explanations to do with adding too much Fire essence during the process of creating steel run afoul of cast iron's inclusion in the list of faerie banes. <br /> <br /> The only consistent difference is in the way human culture thinks and speaks about iron and steel. Steel is a technological marvel that represents the current peak of metallurgy, and its production is regarded as an arcane process. There's an element of wonder there. <br /> <br /> So you have iron. It's the bane of the Fair Folk, for whatever reason. I don't think the &amp;quot;why&amp;quot; of the iron bane was ever the main point under discussion, so we'll take it as read. Some innate property of iron makes it baneful to raksha. You melt and cast it, still baneful. Heat and hammer it any way you like, alloy it with whatever, as long as it's still predominantly iron, still baneful. So steel should count, yes? Yes. Except that because of cultural attitudes to steel, for the purposes of the raksha what you're doing when you create steel, with those cutting edge production methods that produce this marvelously useful and valuable material, is alloying iron not just with carbon but with <i>human wonder</i>. And that shit is delicious. That's what deprives steel of its banefulness. <br /> <br /> What I wonder is, in that case, would steel become a bane again when it becomes easier to produce and more widespread, and thus less wondrous? Or does its history as a marvelous metal of arcane craftsmanship mean its bane status is lost forever?</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh, had I read through the thread fully I would have found an answer that says what I was trying to say, but more effectively.<br /> <br /> On the point of steel becoming mundane, I imagine steel as an idea will continue to be exceptional and iron continue not to be (in a bane to Fae sort of way) regardpess of how human thought or society changes because I believe it was iron as cast and not iron as conceived. In the same way that the infinite possibilities of the Wyld are cast into the (semi)-limited form of the Raksha when they interact with Creation, I imagine the bane is intrinsically linked to the theo-physics (ugh, this is inadvertantly very late 2nd edition of me =\\ ) of their nature and the nature of reality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron-crowns.obsidianportal.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron...dianportal.com</a> Visit Golden Shackles, Iron Crowns; a 3rd Edition Exalted Campaign ongoing since 2014","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2711-janissary87","name":"Janissary87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2711_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T09:34:56-07:00","id":1230567,"title":"","content":"Honestly, the OP says it's nonsense, but to me the whole &quot;iron is much more associated with building and cold materialism while steel is much more associated with killing people&quot; thing that someone mentioned at the beginning rings true enough to me to use it. It might be a cultural thing, but I know that in my language, if you have a poem talking of &quot;cold iron&quot;, it's most likely going for associations of impersonalness, duration, unfeeling - iron doesn't give a shit. But if a poem employs the words &quot;cold steel&quot; at all, someone is getting murdered before the stanza is over, end of. &quot;Steel&quot; is often used as practically a kenning for &quot;weapon&quot; in more florid poetic language. So for me at least, playing in my language, it works, and I'm stealing the shit out of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1793-drascin","name":"Drascin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1793_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"400"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T10:01:08-07:00","id":1230574,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1230525#post1230525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You mean like how ghosts are vulnerable to salt, but this doesn't mean a lot now without particular charms/techniques?</div> </div> </div> </div>More it feels that the number of spirits with a particular bane hasn't increased much since early 1e. There's a good range of behavioural quirks that could be taken advantage of by someone versed in the occult but it feels like they were increasingly written under the assertion that the PCs would probably tear through them like tissue paper anyway.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-27T19:05:41-07:00","id":1230716,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1230525#post1230525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You mean like how ghosts are vulnerable to salt, but this doesn't mean a lot now without particular charms/techniques?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not even that they're vulnerable to it; they just can't cross a line of it in the earth.<br /> <br /> (Or sprouted grain.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T02:28:53-07:00","id":1230761,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Janissary87</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1230530#post1230530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always assumed the weakness was to wrought iron was a part and parcel of the Creation Spanning Trade Network... no, that's not its name, but the name escapese me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Order Conferring Trade Pattern. The conceit that active trade helps stabilish the Pattern .<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-28T04:56:04-07:00","id":1230769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greyman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1230761#post1230761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Order Conferring Trade Pattern. The conceit that active trade helps stablish the Pattern .</div> </div> </div> </div>That's it, thanks! I think it's the idea that trade, like other forms of infrastructure and bureaucracy and civilization, speak to a truth of universal compromise. The existence of the Raksha is to dominate the story or compromise themselves to some other entities greater story. To compromise themselves to a story that doesn't center on a character but an era, a horizon or a society? Blasphemy. <br /> <br /> Under this understanding, wrought iron is a symbol made real, and thereby a bane. <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"> \u00af\\_(\u30c4)_/\u00af At least in my mind.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron-crowns.obsidianportal.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://exalted-golden-shackles-iron...dianportal.com</a> Visit Golden Shackles, Iron Crowns; a 3rd Edition Exalted Campaign ongoing since 2014","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2711-janissary87","name":"Janissary87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2711_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-11T09:06:50-07:00","id":1234023,"title":"","content":"In distant and forgotten days, Luna had a highly spirited conversation with Shaitali, The Rusted Crown, an aspect of Dharma, Shinma of Failure.<br /> <br /> &quot;What is the meaning of your name?&quot; she asked.<br /> <br /> &quot;I embody that which sees even the most resolute things brought to ruin. The madness of kings, the deaths of empires and the grinding of mountains to dust, all are within me.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Just so? But I still do not take your meaning. I know of crowns, surely, but what is &quot;rust&quot;? Of course, Luna knew, and Shaitali knew that she knew, but its pride was great, and so it answered with great enthusiasm.<br /> <br /> &quot;Rust is the bane of iron, it is the triumph of formless things - of wind and water - over the calcified nature of Shape. Is that not a fine thing?<br /> <br /> &quot;Ah, and so iron must be the most Shaped of things, yes?&quot;<br /> <br /> Shaitali, The Rusted Crown, could give only one answer without making a lesser thing of itsel<b>f.</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24387,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24387-amayad","user":"Amayad","date":". 07-11-2018, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24387-amayad","name":"Amayad","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"137"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-26T12:07:42-07:00","id":1239013,"title":"","content":"So bit of necromancy here. So the new Rhaksha antagonist from Adversaries brings an interesting point in with his new merit. Basically he doesn't have the iron weakness the fey in the core do instead having a bane to individuals with linguistics 0. I feel like that means something for the whole concept of the weakness. Particularly as Adiemantus's weakness is described as, &quot;To those who neglect the history drawn upon his skin.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-26T17:16:27-07:00","id":1239088,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1225276-steel-and-iron-what-s-the-difference?p=1239013#post1239013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So bit of necromancy here. So the new Rhaksha antagonist from Adversaries brings an interesting point in with his new merit. Basically he doesn't have the iron weakness the fey in the core do instead having a bane to individuals with linguistics 0. I feel like that means something for the whole concept of the weakness. Particularly as Adiemantus's weakness is described as, &quot;To those who neglect the history drawn upon his skin.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not something that I would have thought of, and if anybody here had suggested it I would probably have argued against it, but since it's a detail now I'm going to consider interesting implications that it might have.<br /> <br /> Something that I've been wondering about for a while is the difference between Raksha nobles and commoners, since the latter category is not so broad as it once was (in the prior Editions, it seemed to awkwardly cover both the full elfin folk, as well as all of the servile races such as hobgoblins). I was inclined to see the templates in the core as referring to the lesser Raksha; still the highest race of the old Wyld tribes, but not the major players. <br /> <br /> And still possibly something unified by an element of being a kind of folk story spectre, hence a general generic weakness to a dull and mundane talisman.<br /> <br /> Adiemantus suggests that this take on the noble Raksha is more than just one with two ruling Graces, higher Attribute caps, and implicitly more personal sophistication; that each will be a more unique entity, shaped by and expressing whatever mythos of preying upon or appropriating Creation that they can acquire, and thus their weakness comes from something particularly contrary to that.<br /> <br /> If nothing else, it seems like a more grounded and structured way of implementing the idea of them crystallizing around a personal narrative, which actually invests it with more versatility.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}